://takuapa101.com/places/rong-jae/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">เรื่องเล่า</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">แผ่นพับ</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/rong-jae/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Shrines & Vegetarian Halls</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Shrines and vegetarian halls</span><h1>The Shrine in the Garden</h1><p class="english">The Shrine in the Garden</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">6 items verified</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="rong-jae">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">ดูทริปของคุณ</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/rong-jae.webp" alt="โรงเจในสวน" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><a href="https://roijang.com/phangnga/โกสุ้ย-ขนมพื้นเมือ-งตะกั่วป่า-พังงา-2023-12-6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>An old shrine of the Chinese community in Takua Pa</h3><p class="era-years">The exact construction year is unknown; municipal documents state that it is more than a century old</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Rong Jae Nai Suan, or Seng Leng Keng Shrine, appears in Takua Pa Town Municipality documents as a centuries-old shrine, but the same documents do not give its construction year. It is therefore not yet possible to place its beginnings in a particular reign.</p><p>The founder identified in municipal documents is Mr So Thian Seng, an overseas Chinese settler. After the shrine was completed, Phra Cheng Chui Cho Sue, or Chao Pho Yat Nam Khang, was brought from China and enshrined there. The place therefore likely served both as a site of worship and as a centre of belief for the Chinese community in the mining and port town of Takua Pa. However, there is no site-specific evidence for its construction year, the founder's policy rationale, or its everyday role at the time.</p><p>Another local document says that Mr So Seng had worked with Ku Chai Tung Shrine before opening the shrine in the garden. However, it cannot yet be confirmed that he was the same person as Mr So Thian Seng, so these are not presented as the same fact.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality): Environmental Operation Plan</a> · <a href="https://anyflip.com/tshcu/hnol/basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Takua Pa Vegetarian Festival, สภาวัฒนธรรมตำบลตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Sub-district Cultural Council)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>A transition period with blank documents</h3><p class="era-years">No confirmed year or event has been found</p><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p>Among the verifiable sources, no evidence has been found that the shrine changed ownership, was abandoned, or was restored in any particular year. No site-specific evidence has been found showing how the 2004 tsunami (พ.ศ. 2547) affected this building.</p><p>No historic-site registration entry has been found for Rong Jae Nai Suan, and there is no information that it was converted to another business or type of public building. The verifiable information for the intervening period is therefore limited to the continuity of the place name and memories of an old shrine. The history of another shrine in the old town should not be used to fill the gaps.</p><p>Takua Pa's context changed from a mining and maritime-trading town into a historic and cultural town, but the sources found do not yet connect this city-level change with specific events at Rong Jae Nai Suan.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.takuacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality): Environmental Operation Plan</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>Still named in the Vegetarian Festival landscape, but operational status is unclear</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p>Currently, verifiable online sources do not confirm who manages the shrine, whether it is open to visitors every day, what condition the building is in, or whether any restoration project is under way. It should therefore not be described as an open tourist attraction or as a place that can be visited without an appointment.</p><p>The contemporary evidence found is a review article reporting the sale of ko sui sweets at Rong Jae Nai Suan during the Vegetarian Festival. This indicates that the place was still used for a community festival activity, but it is a blog report, not a shrine schedule announcement, and does not confirm that the activity is held every year.</p><p>Travellers should use the Soi Rong Phra, Thanon Rat Bamrung address as a starting point and check with local people or the municipality before travelling, because no verified coordinates, opening hours, telephone number, or official contact channel are available.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://roijang.com/phangnga/โกสุ้ย-ขนมพื้นเมือ-งตะกั่วป่า-พังงา-2023-12-6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Roi Jang: Ko Sui, Takua Pa local dessert</a> · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Takua Pa Town Municipality: Place Information Document</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Founding of the shrine</h3><p>The municipality document identified the founder as Mr. So Thian Seng, an overseas Chinese, but did not specify the founding year.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Inviting the deity from China</h3><p>After building the shrine, Phra Cheng Chui Cho Sue, or the Dewdrop God, was invited from China to be enshrined.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Kosui snack activity in the Vegetarian Festival</h3><p>A review article reported the selling of Kosui snacks at the Shrine in the Garden, but it is not an official announcement and it is not verified if it will be held every year.</p><a href="https://roijang.com/phangnga/โกสุ้ย-ขนมพื้นเมือ-งตะกั่วป่า-พังงา-2023-12-6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Deity Cheng Chui Cho Sue</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The deity that the municipality states is enshrined in the shrine. Also known as the Dewdrop Deity, it has a history of being invited from China.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Ko Sui snacks during the Vegetarian Festival</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The Roi Jang blog reports that Grandma Tan Si Lun sells Ko Sui snacks at the Shrine in the Garden during the Vegetarian Festival. This is information from a blog that still needs to be verified for sales in the year of travel.</p><a href="https://roijang.com/phangnga/โกสุ้ย-ขนมพื้นเมือ-งตะกั่วป่า-พังงา-2023-12-6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The name "Rong Chae Nai Suan" (Shrine in the Garden) is a common name for Seng Leng Keng Shrine, according to Takua Pa Town Municipality data.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Municipal documents mention a reputation for traditional Chinese healing. This is the history of the place's beliefs, not evidence of medical efficacy.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li><a href="/en/places/guan-yu/">Guan Yu / Sin Chai Tung Shrine</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/pun-thao/">Pun Thao Kong A Shrine</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/kue-chai/">Kue Chai Tung Shrine</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>Takua Pa Town Municipality's environmental action plan documents say that Rong Jae Nai Suan is called Seng Leng Keng Shrine and is more than a century old, without specifying an exact construction year. The municipality identifies its founder as Mr So Thian Seng, an overseas Chinese settler.</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>The deity that the municipality says is enshrined at the shrine is also known as Chao Pho Yat Nam Khang and is said to have been brought from China. The Hroi Jang blog reports that Ama Tan Si Lun sells ko sui sweets at Rong Jae Nai Suan during the Vegetarian Festival. This is blog information, and sales in the year of travel still need to be checked.</p></details><details><summary>How the municipality document mentions this place</summary><p>A place that documents connect with Chinese traditions and cultural continuity</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Town Municipality · Environmental Action Plan for Fiscal Year 2020 (พ.ศ. 2563)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Vegetarian Festival, Takua Pa Subdistrict Cultural Council · Copy on AnyFlip</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://anyflip.com/tshcu/hnol/basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Hroi Jang Phang Nga · Ko Sui, a local sweet from Takua Pa, Phang Nga</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://roijang.com/phangnga/โกสุ้ย-ขนมพื้นเมือ-งตะกั่วป่า-พังงา-2023-12-6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality): Environmental Operation Plan</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Vegetarian Festival, สภาวัฒนธรรมตำบลตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Sub-district Cultural Council)</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://anyflip.com/tshcu/hnol/basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Roi Jang: Ko Sui, Takua Pa local dessert</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://roijang.com/phangnga/โกสุ้ย-ขนมพื้นเมือ-งตะกั่วป่า-พังงา-2023-12-6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The English name is used according to the brief. No source confirming this as the official English name has been found yet; SEO keywords are suggestions for searching, not search volume data.</li><li>No coordinates that can verify its identity have been found from Google Maps, OpenStreetMap, Mapcarta, or Wikidata.</li><li>A Google Map embedded in the Roi Jang article shows the name Rong Than Shrine Hiap Thian Tai Te, Thung Yai Shrine, Phang Nga Province, which does not match the Shrine in the Garden, so the coordinates from that map are not used.</li><li>The address is only known as Soi Rong Phra, Rat Bamrung Road; no specific building number, subdistrict, and postal code confirmed for the location have been found.</li><li>No verified opening hours and admission fees have been found, so it is not assumed that it is open every day or free.</li><li>No verified phone number, Facebook page, website, or LINE account for the shrine has been found.</li><li>No direct travel information to the shrine from Takua Pa town center, Phuket, or Khao Lak, including distances and public transport, has been found.</li><li>No exact construction year or restoration year has been found. The phrase "century-old" is not enough to calculate the construction year.</li><li>No specific evidence regarding the role of the Shrine in the Garden in the tin mining business has been found.</li><li>No verifiable description of this building's architecture has been found, so it is not stated as Sino-Portuguese or detailed with Chinese decorations.</li><li>No ancient monument registration announcement specific to the Shrine in the Garden has been found.</li><li>Founder information in the book on AnyFlip uses the name So Seng, while the municipality uses So Thian Seng, and a connection between the individuals cannot yet be made.</li><li>Only two highlights with direct site evidence were found. The sweets item is seasonal information from a blog, so no unsupported items have been added to reach 3·6 points.</li><li>No month and lunar calendar specific to this shrine have been found, nor the 2026 schedule.</li><li>No visiting duration, dress code, parking, restrooms, ramps, or accessibility information for the elderly and wheelchairs have been found.</li><li>No verified walking routes from the shrine to nearby places have been found.</li><li>No official image link verifying it is the Shrine in the Garden and whose image can be checked has been found.</li><li>The newly referenced municipal document is the Environmental Operations Plan for Fiscal Year 2020.</li><li>No verified construction year, restoration year, or founding date has been found.</li><li>No evidence of changing hands, abandonment, other uses, effects from the 2004 tsunami, or site-specific ancient monument registration has been found.</li><li>No current caretaker, opening hours, building condition, development projects, coordinates, phone number, and official contact channels have been found.</li><li>No old or current photos that verify the identity of the Shrine in the Garden from readable sources have been found.</li><li>No news from the past five years specific to the Shrine in the Garden has been found.</li><li>The context of the tin mining era, maritime trade, and the Chinese community of Takua Pa still has no evidence tying it directly to specific events at this location.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\places\guan-yu\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Guan Yu / Sin Chai Tung Shrine, Takua Pa · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="The red doors and the town&#x27;s faith · Guan Yu Shrine Takua Pa · Sin Chai Tueng Shrine · Takua Pa vegetarian festival"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Guan Yu / Sin Chai Tung Shrine, Takua Pa"><meta property="og:description" content="The red doors and the town&#x27;s faith · Guan Yu Shrine Takua Pa · Sin Chai Tueng Shrine · Takua Pa vegetarian festival"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/places/guan-yu/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/guan-yu.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/guan-yu.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/guan-yu/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "หน้าแรก", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "ศาลเจ้าพ่อกวนอู ซิ่นไช่ตึ๋ง ตะกั่วป่า", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/places/guan-yu/"}]}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "TouristAttraction", "name": "ศาลเจ้าพ่อกวนอู ซิ่นไช่ตึ๋ง", "url": "https://takuapa101.com/places/guan-yu/", "image": "https://takuapa101.com/assets/guan-yu.webp", "description": "ศาลเจ้าที่สัมพันธ์กับความเชื่อในเทพกวนอูและชุมชนการค้า", "address": {"@type": "PostalAddress", "streetAddress": "บริเวณถนนอุดมธาราตัดถนนศรีตะกั่วป่า ย่านตลาดใหญ่ ตำบลตะกั่วป่า อำเภอตะกั่วป่า จังหวัดพังงา", "addressLocality": "ตะกั่วป่า", "addressRegion": "พังงา", "postalCode": "82110", "addressCountry": "TH"}, "geo": {"@type": "GeoCoordinates", "latitude": 8.82945, "longitude": 98.36518}}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "สถานที่นี้มีประวัติความเป็นมาอย่างไร", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "เทศบาลระบุว่าศาลเจ้าเกิดจากศรัทธาของชาวจีนฮกเกี้ยนในตะกั่วป่า มีอายุนับร้อยปี และเป็นที่ยึดเหนี่ยวจิตใจผ่านความเชื่อในกวนอูและเจ้าแม่กวนอิม เทศบาลระบุศาลเจ้าแห่งนี้เป็นต้นกำเนิดเทศกาลกินผักตะกั่วป่า"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "มีจุดเด่นอะไรให้ชม", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "สิ่งสักการะสำคัญที่เทศบาลระบุไว้สำหรับศาลเจ้าแห่งนี้ จีเพลสระบุว่ามีเกี้ยวสำหรับใช้ในขบวนแห่ ต้องตรวจสอบหน้างานว่าเปิดให้ชมในวันเดินทางหรือไม่"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "เอกสารเทศบาลกล่าวถึงที่นี่อย่างไร", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "ศาลเจ้าที่สัมพันธ์กับความเชื่อในเทพกวนอูและชุมชนการค้า"}}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/guan-yu/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/guan-yu/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/guan-yu/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">เรื่องเล่า</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">แผ่นพับ</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/guan-yu/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Shrines & Vegetarian Halls</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Shrines and vegetarian halls</span><h1>Guan Yu / Sin Chai Tung Shrine</h1><p class="english">Guan Yu / Sin Chai Tung Shrine</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified 4 items</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="guan-yu">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">ดูทริปของคุณ</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/guan-yu.webp" alt="ศาลเจ้าพ่อกวนอู ซิ่นไช่ตึ๋ง" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.82945, 98.36518 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.82945,98.36518" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://mapcarta.com/W816145901" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd>Reviews mention a public toilet near the shrine, but its current availability and facilities inside have not been verified <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd>Community sources note that vegetarian festival participants wear white; no dress code requirements are found for normal days. <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9670000093105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a><a href="https://wanderlog.com/place/details/10219897/sin-chai-tueng-shrine" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#news">News and updates</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#getting_there">Getting there</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>Guan Yu faith in the mining town: The origins of the South Market Shrine</h3><p class="era-years">1843 CE (an oral account; not verified)</p><span class="fact-status disputed">Conflicting information</span><p>An account recorded by Gplace says that Hokkien Chinese people who came to mine in Khlong Pi community joined together to build a Guan Yu shrine, with Lim Bun Tuek involved in its founding, and began a nine-day, nine-night Vegetarian Festival ritual in 1843 CE. The same source spells personal names inconsistently, and no primary evidence has been found, so this should be read as an origin legend, not a verified year</p><p>At that time, Takua Pa was a coastal town growing through tin mining and Hokkien Chinese merchant networks. The shrine therefore likely served both as a home for the community's protective deity and as a ritual centre for miners and merchants. This is consistent with the municipality's description that the shrine arose from Hokkien Chinese faith and was the origin of Takua Pa's Vegetarian Festival, but the year and builder have not been verified in academic documents.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gplace</a> · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality)</a> · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/951" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Takua Pa Town</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>From a community shrine to the South Market Shrine in the old town</h3><p class="era-years">1902 CE (an oral account; not verified) · before 2024 CE</p><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p>Gplace says the shrine was moved from Khlong Pi community to a permanent site at Ban Chae Lim in Talat Yai, and that funds were raised to buy a house in 1902 CE by Lim Eng Cheng, or Tao Kae Pho Daeng. However, no evidence has been found confirming the building date or transfer of ownership, so that year should not be treated as the construction year of the current shrine</p><p>The evidence found describes continuity in the site's role rather than a change of function: the shrine remains tied to Takua Pa's Vegetarian Festival, which has been passed down among Thai people of Chinese descent on the Andaman coast, and later became part of the Talat Yai old-town landscape. At the same time, no online evidence has been found confirming any restoration, abandonment, direct impact from the 2004 tsunami, or registration of this shrine as a historic site.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gplace</a> · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/951" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Takua Pa Town</a> · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Talat Yai</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>A shrine still breathing with the vegetarian festival</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The latest news evidence found confirms the shrine's use as Talat Tai's vegetarian-festival shrine: on 2 October 2024, a Ko Teng pole-raising ceremony was held there to begin the Vegetarian Festival. The hanging of nine lanterns and continuing ceremonies show that the shrine remains a centre of community faith and shared activities, not merely an old building in the market area</p><p>For 2026, no source has confirmed opening hours, a custodian, admission fees, the building's latest condition, or a restoration project specifically for the shrine. Contact the community or municipality before travelling. Based on the information available, the shrine is near the junction of Thanon Udom Thara and Thanon Si Takua Pa in the Talat Yai area, and the confirmed activities are the Vegetarian Festival and the Ko Teng pole-raising ceremony.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9670000093105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Manager Online</a> · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Talat Yai</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">1843 CE (2386 BE)</p><h3>Story of the shrine's founding and the start of the vegetarian festival</h3><p>Gplace states that the Guan Yu shrine was founded and the 9-day vegetarian festival started in the Khlong Pi community, but there is no primary evidence to verify this.</p><a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">1902 CE (2445 BE)</p><h3>Story of permanent relocation to Talat Yai</h3><p>Gplace notes fundraising to buy a house to relocate the shrine to Talat Yai, but it is not verified if this was the year the current building was constructed.</p><a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">2 October 2024</p><h3>Go Teng pole raising at Rong Phra Talat Tai</h3><p>News reports the Go Teng pole raising ceremony to start the Takua Pa people's vegetarian festival.</p><a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9670000093105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Guan Yu and Guanyin statues</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Key objects of worship noted by the municipality for this shrine.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Two architectural styles</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Gplace mentions a Sino-Portuguese building and a Chinese shrine building on opposite sides of the road, though the styles are not verified by architectural surveys.</p><a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Deity palanquins</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Gplace notes palanquins used in processions, but availability for viewing should be checked on-site.</p><a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="news"><h2>News and updates</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2024-10-02 · MGR Online</p><h3>An old shrine in Takua Pa district, Phang Nga province, has performed the Go Teng pole raising ceremony</h3><p>Reports on the Go Teng pole raising ceremony at Guan Yu (Sin Chai Tung) Shrine, Rong Phra Talat Tai, to begin the vegetarian festival.</p><a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9670000093105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The Takua Pa Town Municipality states that this shrine is the origin of the Takua Pa vegetarian festival.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Another name used for this shrine is Rong Phra Talat Tai (South Market Shrine).</p><a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9670000093105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="getting_there"><h2>Getting there</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa Town</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The destination is in the Talat Yai district, near the intersection of Udom Thara and Sri Takua Pa roads. No verified bus routes from the new town to the shrine have been found.</p><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Phuket</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>A transport-information website lists Route 436 Phuket·Takua Pa, passing through Khok Kloi, Thai Mueang, and Khao Lak before reaching Takua Pa Bus Terminal. This information does not verify current departures or onward transport to the shrine</p><a href="https://gogo.town/route/phuket-takuapa-bus-436/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Khao Lak</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Khao Lak is on Route 436 Phuket·Takua Pa according to a transport-information website. Check boarding points and departures that take passengers en route; no information has been found on onward transport from the bus terminal to the shrine</p><a href="https://gogo.town/route/phuket-takuapa-bus-436/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li>Old Town Wall <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://mapcarta.com/W816145901" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li>Wat Na Mueang <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://mapcarta.com/W816145901" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/kue-chai/">Kue Chai Tung Shrine</a> <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></li><li>Wat Sena Nucharangsan <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://mapcarta.com/W816145901" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/pun-thao/">Pun Thao Kong A Shrine</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/rong-jae/">The Shrine in the Garden</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>The municipality states that the shrine grew from the faith of Hokkien Chinese people in Takua Pa, is hundreds of years old, and serves as a spiritual anchor through belief in Guan Yu and Guanyin. The municipality identifies this shrine as the origin of Takua Pa's Vegetarian Festival</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>The municipality lists important objects of worship at this shrine. Gplace says it has a palanquin used in processions. Check on site whether it is open for viewing on the day of travel</p></details><details><summary>How the municipality document mentions this place</summary><p>A shrine associated with belief in the deity Guan Yu and the trading community</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Municipality: Environmental Action Plan, document page 14</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre: Talat Yai community database</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">MGR Online: Ko Teng pole-raising ceremony, 2 October 2024</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9670000093105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Mapcarta: Sin Chai Tung Shrine</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://mapcarta.com/W816145901" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Gplace: Guan Yu Shrine, Sin Chai Tung</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Wanderlog: Sin Chai Tung Shrine</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wanderlog.com/place/details/10219897/sin-chai-tueng-shrine" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">GogoTown: Route 436 Phuket·Takua Pa</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://gogo.town/route/phuket-takuapa-bus-436/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Takua Pa Town</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/951" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Talat Yai</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Manager Online</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9670000093105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Gplace</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">1672 TravelBuddy</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://1672travelbuddy.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>Coordinates are based on Mapcarta referencing OpenStreetMap, giving moderate confidence as it is unverified whether it points to the entrance or a specific building.</li><li>Wanderlog lists Bang Sai subdistrict, which differs from Gplace and the Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre's community database. Takua Pa subdistrict is used based on the latter. No building number was found.</li><li>Opening·closing hours and admission fee are null because no verifiable notice was found</li><li>Phone number is null: Wanderlog shows +66 82 652 9164, but no shrine or official source verifies it as current.</li><li>Facebook is null: Gplace provides a broken link. No official website or LINE account was found.</li><li>Verified construction years and founders remain undiscovered. The years 1843 CE and 1902 CE are narratives from Gplace and should not be treated as direct building years.</li><li>Connections to the tin smelter are found only in stories and cannot directly confirm roles regarding mine workers or trade networks.</li><li>No Fine Arts Department documents or Royal Gazette announcements indicating historical site registration for this shrine have been found. Mention of Ministry of Interior registration in Gplace is not proof of historical site status.</li><li>Information on parking, ramps, door widths, steps, or wheelchair-accessible restrooms was not found, so accessibility for the elderly is not evaluated.</li><li>Recommended visiting times for general viewing and verified visit durations were not found.</li><li>Distances from origins to the shrine, transit routes, and walking times to nearby places are unverified. Nearby listings are based on maps, not pedestrian surveys.</li><li>A verified festival schedule for 2026 CE has not been found.</li><li>Official image links with verified sources were not found.</li><li>SEO keywords are suggestions based on names and content, not actual search volume data.</li><li>The English name follows the prompt, while varying spellings exist in Gplace and Mapcarta. An official English name announced by the shrine has not been found.</li><li>There is no primary evidence for the BE founding year or the current building's construction year.</li><li>The names of founders and caretakers across different eras remain unverified.</li><li>Evidence of restoration, abandonment, or alternative uses has not been found.</li><li>Evidence that the shrine was directly affected by the 2004 CE tsunami has not been found.</li><li>Evidence of the shrine being registered as a historical site has not been found.</li><li>Opening hours, building conditions, caretakers, and the 2026 CE festival schedule remain unverified.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\traditions\new-year-alms\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>New Year&#x27;s Dawn Almsgiving, Takua Pa · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="A merit-making activity to welcome the new year · Takuapa New Year alms · Takuapa morning alms · New Year tradition Takuapa"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="New Year&#x27;s Dawn Almsgiving, Takua Pa"><meta property="og:description" content="A merit-making activity to welcome the new year · Takuapa New Year alms · Takuapa morning alms · New Year tradition Takuapa"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/traditions/new-year-alms/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/new-year-alms.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/new-year-alms.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/traditions/new-year-alms/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Morning Almsgiving for the New Year", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/traditions/new-year-alms/"}]}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguag