on.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/governor-wall/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/governor-wall/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/governor-wall/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">เรื่องเล่า</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">แผ่นพับ</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/governor-wall/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Old Buildings & City Traces</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Old buildings and city traces</span><h1>Governor’s Residence Wall</h1><p class="english">Governor’s Residence Wall</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">15 items verified</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="governor-wall">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">ดูทริปของคุณ</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/governor-wall.webp" alt="กำแพงจวนเจ้าเมืองตะกั่วป่า" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.830904, 98.366634 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.830904,98.366634" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd>Open every day (hours not specified) <span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd>No admission fee <span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#news">News and updates</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#getting_there">Getting there</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>The governor's camp wall in a tin-mining town by the sea route</h3><p class="era-years">1840·1881 (B.E. 2383·2424)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The Takua Pa Governor's Residence Wall was probably built during the tenure of Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon), governor of Takua Pa, around 1840·1881 (B.E. 2383·2424), but academic sources have not identified a definite construction start year. The wall enclosed the residence and administrative area, functioning like a fortification to protect against danger and provide shelter for townspeople</p><p>This was the period when Takua Pa grew through its tin-mining economy and coastal trade. The old town lies along the Takua Pa River, which connects to the Andaman Sea and multinational trade networks. Chinese communities involved in mining and trade played an important role, but conflicts over mining interests made a secure protected area necessary. The wall was therefore both the ruler's residence and a means of maintaining order in the town</p><p>The interior layout was divided into the front palace, hall, and rear palace, connected by doorways. It was built with layers of mortar mixed with gravel and sand, on a base wider than the wall itself. The surviving sections are about 5 metres high. Later excavations found Qing-dynasty Chinese ceramics, European ceramics, and contemporary materials, reflecting the town's trade contacts at the time</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ฐานข้อมูลแหล่งโบราณคดีที่สำคัญในประเทศไทย (ศมส.) (Important Archaeological Sites in Thailand Database, SAC)</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานสิ่งแวดล้อมศิลปกรรม (Cultural Environment Office) ONEP: Takua Pa Arts and Cultural Environments</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>From a lived-in residence to a historical site protected by the old town</h3><p class="era-years">1881·2025 (B.E. 2424·2568)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>After Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch) left office, the wall and residence continued to be used during the tenure of Phraya Senanuchit (Iam Na Nakhon), from 1881·1892 (B.E. 2424·2435). King Chulalongkorn visited Takua Pa and viewed this residence on 28 May R.S. 109, or 1890 (B.E. 2433)</p><p>Later, when the town was relocated to Ko Kho Khao and Ban Yan Yao during the tenure of Phraya Narathipphakdi (Sit Na Nakhon), the former residence lost its official role and was probably abandoned from that period. The verifiable sources found no evidence confirming that the wall was used for another purpose or directly damaged by the 2004 tsunami (B.E. 2547)</p><p>The Fine Arts Department (Fine Arts Department) registered it as an archaeological monument and defined its land boundary in the Royal Gazette, Volume 113, Special Section 50 Ngor, dated 18 December 1996 (B.E. 2539). It was later cleared and excavated in 1999 (B.E. 2542), followed by restoration and landscaping in later periods. Current sources state that the wall is more stable, but some areas still need care because of weeds and roots</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ฐานข้อมูลแหล่งโบราณคดีที่สำคัญในประเทศไทย (ศมส.) (Important Archaeological Sites in Thailand Database, SAC)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>A living historical site: the old wall becomes Takua Pa's stage</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Today, the Takua Pa Governor's Residence Wall is an archaeological monument open to visitors every day free of charge. An archaeological-site database lists the joint custodians as the Fine Arts Department (Fine Arts Department), Takua Pa Municipality (Takua Pa Municipality), and the Na Nakhon family. The grounds include a lawn and some walkways, with information signs from the Fine Arts Department and the Tourism Authority of Thailand (Tourism Authority of Thailand)</p><p>The site no longer serves as the governor's residence, but it remains a landmark in the old town and a venue for ceremonies and community activities. In 2026 (B.E. 2569), Phang Nga Province (Phang Nga Province) and Takua Pa Municipality (Takua Pa Municipality) held the event “From Takola to Takua Pa, 12th edition” at the residence from 28 February·3 March. It featured khon, nora, and nang talung performances, blessing and Buddhist chanting ceremonies, and sales of local food and OTOP products</p><p>Contemporary use of the site is therefore heritage tourism and learning about life in Takua Pa. At the same time, some sections of the wall remain damaged and have plants growing on them. The verifiable official sources have not announced a new restoration project or specific budget details for 2026 (B.E. 2569)</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ฐานข้อมูลแหล่งโบราณคดีที่สำคัญในประเทศไทย (ศมส.) (Important Archaeological Sites in Thailand Database, SAC)</a> · <a href="https://radiothailand.prd.go.th/th/content/category/detail/id/9/iid/481052" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Broadcasting Services of Thailand (National Broadcasting Services of Thailand): From Takola to Takua Pa, 12th edition</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1840 (B.E. 2383)</p><h3>Began during the time of Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch)</h3><p>It is suggested, not settled, that the construction of the wall surrounding the residence and official area began to defend against enemies and chaos from tin mining conflicts.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1881 (B.E. 2424)</p><h3>The residence was still in use during the next governor's time</h3><p>Phraya Senanuchit (Iam na Nakhon) became the governor, and the use of the residence area continued until 1892, according to documents summarised by the archaeological database.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">28 May R.S. 109</p><h3>King Rama V viewed the governor's residence</h3><p>King Chulalongkorn visited Takua Pa and viewed this governor's residence.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1892 (B.E. 2435)</p><h3>Lost its role when the town centre moved</h3><p>When the town location moved to Ko Khao Island and Ban Yan Yao, the former residence was no longer in use and was likely abandoned from this period.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">18 December 1996 (2539)</p><h3>Registered as a historical site</h3><p>The Fine Arts Department announced its registration and designated the historical area in the Royal Gazette.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1999 (B.E. 2542)</p><h3>Archaeological excavation</h3><p>Qing dynasty Chinese ceramics, European ceramics, pottery fragments, glassware, and architectural decorative pieces were found.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">28 February·3 March 2026 (B.E. 2569)</p><h3>The residence as an old town event space</h3><p>The 12th 'From Takola to Takua Pa' event was held, featuring performances, ceremonies, and a local food and OTOP goods market.</p><a href="https://radiothailand.prd.go.th/th/content/category/detail/id/9/iid/481052" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Ancient wall line</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>See the intact parts of the wall, about 5 meters high, and the sections damaged by time.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Three-part layout</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Observe the division into the front palace, the hall, and the rear palace, complete with connecting doorways.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Construction technique</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Notice the layers of lime mixed with gravel and sand, and the wall's base which is wider than the wall itself.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Information boards</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>There are historical information boards from the Fine Arts Department and the Tourism Authority of Thailand at the front.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Old town context</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Located on Udomthara Road on the west bank of the Takua Pa River and near Wat Na Mueang (a local temple).</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="news"><h2>News and updates</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2026-02-28 · National Broadcasting Services of Thailand (National Broadcasting Services of Thailand)</p><h3>Phang Nga hosts the 12th 'From Takola to Takua Pa' event</h3><p>Takua Pa Municipality (Takua Pa Municipality) held an activity promoting old-town tourism at the Takua Pa Governor's Residence from 28 February·3 March 2026 (B.E. 2569)</p><a href="https://radiothailand.prd.go.th/th/content/category/detail/id/9/iid/481052" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The wall has a rectangular layout and its interior is divided into the front palace, the hall, and the rear palace.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Excavations found both Qing Dynasty Chinese ceramics and European ceramics in the same site.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="getting_there"><h2>Getting there</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa Town</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>From the Takua Pa Town Municipality office, take Rat Bamrung Road, turn right onto Montri 2 Road, then turn left onto Si Takua Pa Road. Go straight for about 260 meters to find the historical site on your left.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Phuket</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>No specific route or distance from Phuket was found in the verified sources.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Khao Lak</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>No specific route or distance from Khao Lak was found in the verified sources.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Airport</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>No specific departure airport or distance was found in the verified sources.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li>Wat Na Mueang <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li>Takua Pa old town market <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li>Khun In building <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/iron-bridge/">Boon Soong Iron Bridge</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/khun-in/">Khun In House</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/tao-ming/">Tao Ming School</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>Admission fee</summary><p>No admission fee</p></details><details><summary>Getting there from Takua Pa Town</summary><p>From the Takua Pa Town Municipality office, take Rat Bamrung Road, turn right onto Montri 2 Road, then turn left onto Si Takua Pa Road. Go straight for about 260 meters to find the historical site on your left.</p></details><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>The site dates archaeologically to 1840·1881 (B.E. 2383·2424). It is suggested that it was built during the tenure of Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon), governor of Takua Pa, as a wall enclosing the residence and protecting against enemies</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>See the surviving wall sections, about 5 metres high, and sections damaged over time. Observe the division into the front palace, hall, and rear palace, with connecting doorways, as well as the layers of mortar mixed with gravel and sand and the wall base, which is wider than the wall itself</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Database of Important Archaeological Sites in Thailand: Takua Pa Governor's Residence Wall</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Town · SAC Community Information Center</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1549" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Artistic Heritage Site Worthy of Conservation: Takua Pa</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Database of Important Archaeological Sites in Thailand (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre)</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">สำนักงานสิ่งแวดล้อมศิลปกรรม (Cultural Environment Office) ONEP: Takua Pa Arts and Cultural Environments</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">สถานีวิทยุกระจายเสียงแห่งประเทศไทย (Radio Thailand)</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://radiothailand.prd.go.th/th/content/category/detail/id/9/iid/481052" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Wongnai: Photos of Takua Pa old-town wall</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.wongnai.com/attractions/403843FU-%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B3%E0%B9%81%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%87%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B7%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%87%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2/photos/b51c676106884f98a0c8334b8584b456" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>No specific postal code was found in the verified sources.</li><li>No definite opening·closing hours were found. Official sources only state that it is open to visitors every day</li><li>No direct phone number, Facebook page, website, or LINE account for the site.</li><li>No verified information on parking fees, restrooms, or accessibility for the elderly or wheelchairs.</li><li>No verified information that the wall is a direct Sino-Portuguese architecture.</li><li>No verified distance or travel time from Phuket, Khao Lak, or the airport.</li><li>No verified images with usage rights found.</li><li>No evidence has been found confirming a single construction-start year, other than the range 1840·1881 BE</li><li>No verified evidence that the wall was directly affected by the 2004 tsunami.</li><li>No specific restoration project or budget details for 2026.</li><li>No verified old photos with usage rights found.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><