aces/kue-chai/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">เรื่องเล่า</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">แผ่นพับ</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/kue-chai/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Shrines & Vegetarian Halls</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Shrines and vegetarian halls</span><h1>Kue Chai Tung Shrine</h1><p class="english">Kue Chai Tung Shrine</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">9 items verified</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="kue-chai">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">ดูทริปของคุณ</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/kue-chai.webp" alt="ศาลเจ้ากู่ใช่ตึ๋ง" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.8265089587, 98.3647240165 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.8265089587,98.3647240165" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4411" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd>Dress politely when entering for worship. <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><a href="https://anyflip.com/tshcu/hnol/basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a><a href="https://www.thairath.co.th/news/local/south/1105465" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#news">News and updates</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#getting_there">Getting there</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>From Mr. Ngo Chok's house to Takua Pa's first shrine</h3><p class="era-years">No verified construction year is available; some contemporary documents state that it is nearly 200 years old</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The starting point with the clearest evidence is not a year, but a community story: Mr. Ngo Chok bought house number 87 on Sri Takua Pa Road and converted it into a shrine, driven by faith after praying for tin mining yields and fortune. Another document refers to its original name as 'Thian Wan Keng' and its legal name as 'Kue Chai Tung'.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://anyflip.com/tshcu/hnol/basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สภาวัฒนธรรมตำบลตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Sub-district Cultural Council), Takua Pa Vegetarian Festival</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4411" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>The shrine grew with the mining town and became a community ritual space</h3><p class="era-years">No year has been found for a change of ownership, restoration, or a tsunami event specifically verified for this site</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The context of the shrine is tied to Hokkien Chinese who immigrated to mine tin in Takua Pa and brought deities from China to be enshrined. Local sources and travel media explain that the shrine is in the old market district, which has old buildings and a cultural street. It therefore serves as more than a place to pray: it is an anchor for the Chinese community and a space for the Vegetarian Festival.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4411" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning)</a> · <a href="https://nced.onep.go.th/opendata/2568/oldtown36_announce/takuapa.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Takua Pa Old Town boundary delineation project</a> · <a href="https://wanderlog.com/th/place/details/11412698/san-chao-ku-chai-tueng" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wanderlog (place information summary/user reviews)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>A northern shrine still alive during the Vegetarian Festival</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The latest evidence found is NBT news published in May 2026 (2569 B.E.), which presents Kue Chai Tung Shrine, or 'Rong Phra Talat Nuea' (the northern market shrine), as a center of faith for Takua Pa people and states that it still preserves its original cultural identity. News of the 2023 festival also confirms that Kue Chai Tung Shrine participated in the Iu Keng ceremony, parading deities around the old town of Takua Pa.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q6K2Zl6700I" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NBT2HD</a> · <a href="https://www.innews.news/news.php?n=48726" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Innews</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">13·21 October 2015 (พ.ศ. 2558)</p><h3>Ko Teng pole raising ceremony in the Vegetarian Festival</h3><p>There is a ceremony to invite the 9 lanterns and the presiding deities of the Vegetarian Festival.</p><a href="https://www.sanook.com/news/1881366/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">23 October 2023 (พ.ศ. 2566)</p><h3>Participating in the Ew Keng street procession around the old town</h3><p>Kue Chai Tung Shrine joined Sin Chai Tung Guan Yu Shrine in the street procession ceremony during the Vegetarian Festival.</p><a href="https://www.innews.news/news.php?n=48726" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">4 May 2026 (พ.ศ. 2569)</p><h3>Appeared in NBT news as a centre of faith</h3><p>NBT news presented the shrine as Rong Phra Talat Nuea and mentioned its history of over 200 years, but did not provide the exact founding year.</p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q6K2Zl6700I" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Mr. Ngo Chok bought a house to make a shrine</h3><p>The Cultural Council documents state that house number 87 on Sri Takua Pa Road was turned into a shrine after praying for mining yields and good fortune.</p><a href="https://anyflip.com/tshcu/hnol/basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Inviting deities from China to be enshrined</h3><p>The shrine is recorded as the first shrine in Takua Pa district. It has the deity Nezha and other deities for worship.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4411" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Chinese deities</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>There is the deity Nezha and other deities for worship.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4411" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Traces of a mining community</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Community documents recount the shrine's connection to praying for good tin yields and profit.</p><a href="https://anyflip.com/tshcu/hnol/basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Bridge crossing ceremony</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>During the Vegetarian Festival, there is a Koi Han ceremony (crossing a bridge to ward off bad luck) and the tying of holy thread for auspiciousness.</p><a href="https://www.thairath.co.th/news/local/south/1105465" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa old town context</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Located in the cultural street and old town district of Takua Pa, which features old buildings and community arts and culture.</p><a href="https://tatmediaassets.com/file/1097396" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="news"><h2>News and updates</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2026-05-04 · NBT2HD</p><h3>Praying for blessings at Kue Chai Tung Shrine, Phang Nga Province</h3><p>NBT news recommended the shrine and its role as a centre of faith for Takua Pa people.</p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q6K2Zl6700I" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2023-10-23 · Innews</p><h3>Fierce finale! Phang Nga Vegetarian Festival procession around Takua Pa city</h3><p>Reported on the Ew Keng ceremony where Kue Chai Tung Shrine joined Sin Chai Tung Shrine.</p><a href="https://www.innews.news/news.php?n=48726" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The name 'Rong Phra Talat Nuea' (northern market shrine) is used to distinguish this shrine from Sin Chai Tung Shrine, or the southern market shrine, in the old town district.</p><a href="https://th.readme.me/p/44374" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>This shrine was originally named Thian Wan Keng, and community documents state its legal name is Kue Chai Tung.</p><a href="https://anyflip.com/tshcu/hnol/basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="getting_there"><h2>Getting there</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa Town</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Located in the old town district of Takua Pa, on Sri Takua Pa Road. The sources read do not specify the distance or detailed route recommendations.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4411" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Phuket</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>No readable sources have been found that specify the direct route or distance to the shrine.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4411" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Khao Lak</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>No readable sources have been found that specify the direct route or distance to the shrine.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4411" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Airport</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>No readable sources have been found that specify the direct route or distance to the shrine.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4411" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li><a href="/en/places/guan-yu/">Guan Yu / Sin Chai Tung Shrine</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/pun-thao/">Pun Thao Kong A Shrine</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/rong-jae/">The Shrine in the Garden</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>How to dress</summary><p>Dress politely when entering for worship.</p></details><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>It is the first shrine in Takua Pa District, with deities invited from China to be enshrined here. According to Cultural Council documents, Ngo Chok bought house number 87 on Thanon Si Takua Pa and turned it into a shrine after praying for good mining yields and good fortune.</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>It houses Na Cha and other deities for worship. Community documents link the shrine to prayers for good ore and profitable mining. During the Vegetarian Festival, the shrine holds the Koi Han ceremony, a bridge-crossing ritual, and a sacred-thread tying ritual for good fortune.</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4411" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">สภาวัฒนธรรมตำบลตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Sub-district Cultural Council), Takua Pa Vegetarian Festival</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://anyflip.com/tshcu/hnol/basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Thairath</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.thairath.co.th/news/local/south/1105465" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">TAT Media Assets</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://tatmediaassets.com/file/1097396" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Old Town boundary delineation project</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://nced.onep.go.th/opendata/2568/oldtown36_announce/takuapa.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Innews</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.innews.news/news.php?n=48726" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">NBT2HD</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q6K2Zl6700I" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Trip.com Moments</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://th.trip.com/moments/detail/takua-pa-14944-125098619/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>No opening and closing times found from verifiable sources.</li><li>Verified admission fees could not be found.</li><li>No official phone number, Facebook page, website, or LINE account for the shrine found.</li><li>No postal code specific to the location found from readable sources.</li><li>No verifiable data on the exact construction year or names of builders.</li><li>No evidence found of registration as an ancient monument by the Fine Arts Department.</li><li>No verifiable data on the Sino-Portuguese or Chinese architectural style of the building itself.</li><li>No verifiable data found for parking, restrooms, ramps, or wheelchair access.</li><li>No list of nearby places with verified walking times found.</li><li>No routes or distances from Phuket, Khao Lak, or the airport that specify this shrine directly found.</li><li>No specific construction year or verifiable evidence of builders other than the Mr. Ngo Chok story found.</li><li>No verifiable evidence of a change of hands, abandonment, or restoration with years found.</li><li>No evidence found that the shrine was affected by the 2004 tsunami.</li><li>No year of ancient monument registration by the Fine Arts Department found.</li><li>No information found on current caretakers, opening times, admission fees, structural condition, or recent restoration projects.</li><li>No old photos or archival photos verified to be this shrine directly found.</li><li>News from the past five years is limited; no news of restorations or awards specific to the location found.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\traditions\loy-krathong\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Loy Krathong, Takua Pa · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="Viewing the town through riverside activities · Loy Krathong Takua Pa · Loy Krathong Phang Nga · Takua Pa floating lantern festival"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Loy Krathong, Takua Pa"><meta property="og:description" content="Viewing the town through riverside activities · Loy Krathong Takua Pa · Loy Krathong Phang Nga · Takua Pa floating lantern festival"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/traditions/loy-krathong/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/loy-krathong.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/loy-krathong.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/traditions/loy-krathong/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Loy Krathong", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/traditions/loy-krathong/"}]}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "Event", "name": "Loy Krathong", "url": "https://takuapa101.com/traditions/loy-krathong/", "image": "https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/loy-krathong.png", "description": "Loy Krathong · Krathong making contest · Stage performances · Pier exhibitions or decorations", "location": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Khlong Takua Pa, Tambon Takua Pa, and some years at Khlong Bang Muang, Tambon Bang Muang"}}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/traditions/loy-krathong/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/traditions/loy-krathong/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/traditions/loy-krathong/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">เรื่องเล่า</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" aria-current="page" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">แผ่นพับ</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/traditions/loy-krathong/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="intro"><a class="eyebrow" href="/en/">Takua Pa 101 / Old Town Guide</a><h1>Loy Krathong</h1><p class="lead">Full moon of the 12th Thai lunar month · Document mentions November</p></div><div class="event-note"><span class="calendar-symbol" aria-hidden="true">✳</span><article><span class="chapter">Periods from document</span><h2>Full moon of the 12th Thai lunar month · Document mentions November</h2><p>This item appears in the activities and traditions section of the Takua Pa Town Municipality brochure. The period above is not yet a verified schedule for the current year.</p><a class="text-link" href="/en/traditions/">View year-round traditions ↗</a></article></div><section class="section"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="chapter">Read more / Open town stories</span><h2>Related places</h2></div><p></p></div><div class="related-grid"><article class="place-card" data-place-card data-category="market" data-name="ตลาดริมน้ำตะกั่วป่า"><a class="place-image" href="/en/places/riverwalk/"><img src="/assets/riverwalk.webp" alt="ตลาดริมน้ำตะกั่วป่า" width="1000" height="1000" loading="lazy"><span class="card-index">14</span><span class="image-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><div class="card-copy"><span class="eyebrow">Markets & Food</span><h3><a href="/en/places/riverwalk/">Takuapa Riverwalk</a></h3><p>Food and life by the river</p></div></article></div></section><aside class="source"><h2>Data Sources</h2><p>Compiled from<a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Takua Pa Town Municipality brochure</a> · Data extracted September 6, 2026 (2569 BE). The document does not specify a publication date. Opening hours, shop status, and current schedules are not verified. Please check with the venue before traveling.</p></aside></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\stories\water-trade\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Thung Tuek and the Takua Pa River Trade · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="The river is not just a backdrop. It is a route, a connector of people, and the determiner of the town&#x27;s fate, which had to relocate due to sedimentation."><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Thung Tuek and the Takua Pa River Trade"><meta property="og:description" content="The river is not just a backdrop. It is a route, a connector of people, and the determiner of the town&#x27;s fate, which had to relocate due to sedimentation."><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/water-trade/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/water-trade/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "หน้าแรก", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "ทุ่งตึกและแม่น้ำตะกั่วป่า เส้นทางการค้าโบราณ", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/water-trade/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/water-trade/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/water-trade/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/water-trade/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">เรื่องเล่า</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">แผ่นพับ</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/water-trade/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>Waterways and trade</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · River and Trade</span><h1>From Thung Tuk to the Takua Pa River: The Day the Waterway Built the Town</h1><p class="lead">The river is not just a backdrop. It is a route, a connector of people, and the determiner of the town's fate, which had to relocate due to sedimentation.</p><p class="story-meta">Updated 2026-09-07 · 7 min read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-port.webp" alt="ภาพเก่า เรือจอดที่ท่าน้ำตะกั่วป่า" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Archive photograph: boats moored at the Takua Pa landing</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>If you stand by the Takua Pa River today, the slow and quiet water might seem like a mere backdrop to the old town landscape. However, looking back into history, this river never slept. It was the main artery that pumped prosperity, people, and resources into the area for centuries.</p><p>Takua Pa town was not located at its current coordinates from the beginning. It shifted and grew along the waterway, moving from the flatland near the estuary to the Talat Yai area, until the day river sediment forced the town centre to relocate once again.</p><p>The Takua Pa River is therefore not just a natural landscape, but the true author of history that brought global maritime commerce to the area, and the determiner of the town's destiny when the waterway became shallow.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">Thung Tuk, the Port Before the Town</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/riverwalk.webp" alt="ตลาดริมน้ำตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takuapa Riverwalk</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>Before the image of Takua Pa town became the brick buildings and Sino-Portuguese commercial districts we see today, the original trade centre of this area was located at Thung Tuk, a flatland near the river mouth. Thung Tuk served as a bustling ancient trading port on the trans-peninsular route.</p><p>This estuary area did not grow in isolation. It was a crucial piece connecting to an extensive maritime trade network. It served as a stopover and goods exchange point for travellers of various nationalities, who relied on ocean currents and seasonal monsoons for navigation.</p><p>The Takua Pa waterway during that era was not solely for local transportation. It was used as a primary trans-peninsular route between the Andaman Sea and the Gulf of Thailand. With a favourable distance between the headwaters on both sides, this route drastically reduced travel time.</p><p>Importantly, using the river route helped navigators avoid the dangers and risks of sailing around the long Strait of Malacca. The waterway therefore acted as a bridge connecting two oceans, continuously bringing people, goods, and cultures into the area. This was the first chapter of the port town, before the centre of prosperity expanded deeper inland along the river.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">Suggested, not settled status of the ancient port</span></div></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Takua Pa town was not located at its current coordinates from the beginning. It shifted and grew along the waterway.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">The Steam Boiler and the Prosperity of Talat Yai</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/iron-bridge.webp" alt="สะพานเหล็กบุญสูง" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Boon Soong Iron Bridge</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>As eras transitioned and maritime trade moved further inland, the town centre relocated to the Talat Yai community during the town's restoration in the reign of King Rama III. This area was surrounded by the river on three sides, making it highly suitable for both town defence and water transportation.</p><p>Takua Pa's water trade continued to flourish and left material traces visible to this day. If you travel to Wat Pathum Thara, or Wat Na Mueang (the town's front temple), in the Talat Yai area, you will find a large steam boiler on display. This metal piece is not mining machinery, but a crucial component of a historical steamboat.</p><p>Phraya Sena Nuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon) partnered with Phraya Wichit Songkhram to purchase this steamboat in 1867 (2410 B.E.) to transport goods and suppress pirates in the waters. It serves as proof of the wealth and heavy traffic volume during that era.</p><p>Although the steamboat later deteriorated and sank in the Takua Pa River, the salvaged steam boiler still stands as a witness. The vibrancy of the Talat Yai community at that time was inextricably linked to the river. The people's way of life relied on the waterway, which functioned as the town's main street.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The temple's role in trade</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">The Day Sediment Accumulated and the Town Had to Move</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/wat-pathum.webp" alt="วัดปทุมธารา (วัดหน้าเมือง)" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>But the river is never static, and nature always plays a role in shaping the town's fate. Over time, sediment from the rapidly growing tin mining industry and natural phenomena accumulated until the Takua Pa River became increasingly shallow.</p><p>This geographical change directly impacted the town's economic structure. Navigation, once convenient, decreased and became more difficult. Large merchant ships that previously brought wealth could no longer dock as easily as before, causing the vibrancy of the Talat Yai community to decline.</p><p>Eventually, the town centre had to relocate from the original Talat Yai area to a new district called Yan Yao in 1913. This relocation was not caused by war or epidemics, but by the limitations of the changed waterway, which was no longer conducive to trade.</p><p>This historical event proves that the river had complete influence over human settlement. The day the water became shallow was the day the town had to pack its bags and move to breathe new life into an area better able to accommodate the changes.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">Date and cause of the town relocation</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">Boonsoong Iron Bridge, the Miners' Connection</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/thung-phra.webp" alt="สวนสาธารณะทุ่งพระโพธิ์" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Thung Phra Pho Park</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>As the town centre transitioned from the commercial port era to the golden age of tin mining, the riverside landscape and infrastructure were modified accordingly. In the past, the Takua Pa Canal was over forty metres wide, presenting a natural barrier that divided the area.</p><p>The width of the waterway meant miners and locals had to row boats back and forth between the old market side and the mining company's area. To solve this difficult commute, especially during the flood season when the current was swift, Mr Juti Boonsoong, founder of the tin dredging company, initiated the construction of a wooden bridge first, allowing people to walk and ride bicycles across.</p><p>Later in 1968, the bridge underwent a major renovation. Steel from decommissioned tin dredges was assembled to rebuild the Khok Khanun Iron Bridge, or as we familiarly know it, the Boonsoong Iron Bridge, spanning over two hundred metres across the river.</p><p>This iron bridge is therefore not just a construction object or an engineering structure. It is a shortcut that reduced travel time for workers, a goods transport route, and a connection for daily life. This landmark tells the story of the transition from the rowboat era to the mining industry era that powerfully sustained the town's economy.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">Water Catchment and Merit-Making at Thung Phra Pho</h2><p>The Takua Pa River not only sustains the economy but is also tied to daily life and how locals cope with nature. Riverside areas like Thung Phra Pho Public Park are clear examples of adapting to coexist with the old town's water system.</p><p>Municipal planning documents indicate that the Thung Phra Pho area is important as water management infrastructure, with projects to dredge the canal and construct a pier. During times when the town faces flooding, this area is also used as an installation point for water pumps to alleviate floods for the community.</p><p>However, beyond the dimension of dealing with natural disasters, Thung Phra Pho is also an open space for cultural activities. On the full moon day of the twelfth lunar month every year, this wide riverside plaza is used as the venue for the municipality's annual Loy Krathong (November lantern-floating festival).</p><p>This diverse usage reflects that the riverside areas were not abandoned after the mining era. Instead, their functions were adapted to become both a town defence mechanism and an activity plaza that brings people together on merit-making days.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-5">Traces Still Flowing in the Present Landscape</h2><p>Today, although the mines have closed and large-scale maritime trade has become merely a past recorded on paper pages, many riverside locations are still alive and harmoniously coexist with the breath of the people in Takua Pa old town.</p><p>The Boonsoong Iron Bridge continues to serve as a community thoroughfare, where locals still walk and ride motorcycles back and forth. At the same time, it has become a landmark where people come to enjoy the view and take photos of the old dredge factory traces still visible from the bridge, or even being used as a filming location for foreign movies.</p><p>Along the canal near Wat Yan Yao (a local temple), a community market area named Takua Pa Riverside Market has been initiated to help revive the old atmosphere, allowing people to experience the riverside way of life once again. There are also seafood festivals held according to the cultural calendar.</p><p>The Takua Pa River continues to flow slowly. It is a guardian of memories from the days when it was a global port town crowded with merchant ships, to the day it became the tranquil viewpoint of the old town we see today.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Takuapa Riverwalk</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="riverwalk">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Boon Soong Iron Bridge</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="iron-bridge">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="wat-pathum">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Thung Phra Pho Park</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="thung-phra">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>Architectural characteristics and town layout details of the Thung Tuk port in the era prior to the tin mining trade</li><li>Contemporary photographs with clear usage rights depicting the congestion of water traffic during the era when steamboats were still operating</li><li>Documents officially recording the day of the relocation to Yan Yao, and the voices of the villagers in the Talat Yai community at that time</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Thung Tuek Ancient Trading Port</cite> · กรมศิลปากร (Fine Arts Department) · Thung Tuek archaeological site article · <a href="https://www.finearts.go.th/architecture/view/35708-ทุ่งตึกเมืองท่าการค้าโบราณ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Takua Pa Old Town Documents</cite> · สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · PDF pages 11-13, 55-56, 58 · <a href="https://nced.onep.go.th/opendata/2568/oldtown36_announce/takuapa.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Archaeological Site Database: Talat Yai Community</cite> · ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) · Community historical data · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Bun Sung Iron Bridge (Khok Khanun)</cite> · ฐานข้อมูลท้องถิ่นภาคใต้ มหาวิทยาลัยสงขลานครินทร์ (Southern Local Database, Prince of Songkla University) · History and overview page · <a href="https://clib.psu.ac.th/southerninfo/content/1/de645890" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Environmental Action Plan Document 2022 (2565 BE)</cite> · Thetsaban Mueang Takua Pa (Takua Pa Town Municipality) · Project Records and Water Management · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_strategy/2022-12_231dcd9d5169573.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Takua Pa Flood News and Water Pump Installation</cite> · MGR Online · Regional News · <a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9560000096203" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Takua Pa Riverside Market, Ruamjai Pracharath</cite> · Krom Kan Kha Phainai Krasuang Phanit (Department of Internal Trade, Ministry of Commerce) · Must-Visit Market Information · <a href="https://www.dit.go.th/FILE/CONTENT_FILE/256202181736250948974.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Morning News: Boon Sung Iron Bridge</cite> · NBT2HD · News Program 30 January 2026 (2569) · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QUZ54DTc0LQ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Seafood Festival Calendar, Takua Pa Riverside Market</cite> · Krasuang Watthanatham (Ministry of Culture) · Cultural Activity Calendar · <a href="https://calendar.m-culture.go.th/events/103651" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">Trans-peninsular distance and the origin of Thung Tuk</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">The distance figures are data from the ONEP report, and the establishment of Thung Tuk refers to the Fine Arts Department's explanation. The website maintains the level of the text according to the original documents and has not verified new conclusions. <a href="https://www.finearts.go.th/architecture/view/35708-ทุ่งตึกเมืองท่าการค้าโบราณ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">Town relocation due to the shallow river</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">The date and reason for the town's relocation in 1913 are compiled data based on the ONEP report, which should be cited from the original source report when used in academic work. <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">Origin of the steam boiler at Wat Na Mueang</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">Information from the Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre database indicates the purchase of the steamboat in 1867, but this piece of evidence does not verify that the temple operated the mining business directly. <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">Thung Tuk, the Port Before the Town</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">The Steam Boiler and the Prosperity of Talat Yai</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">The Day Sediment Accumulated and the Town Had to Move</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">Boonsoong Iron Bridge, the Miners' Connection</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">Water Catchment and Merit-Making at Thung Phra Pho</a></li><li><a href="#section-5">Traces Still Flowing in the Present Landscape</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\places\wat-sena\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan · Wat Mai Kampang · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="Observing the coexistence of Thai and Chinese elements · Wat Mai Kamphaeng · Takuapa old town temple · ancient temple in Takuapa"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan · Wat Mai Kampang"><meta property="og:description" content="Observing the coexistence of Thai and Chinese elements · Wat Mai Kamphaeng · Takuapa old town temple · ancient temple in Takuapa"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-sena/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/wat-sena.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/wat-sena.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/wat-sena/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "หน้าแรก", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "วัดเสนานุชรังสรรค์ (วัดใหม่) ตะกั่วป่า", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-sena/"}]}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "TouristAttraction", "name": "วัดเสนานุชรังสรรค์ (วัดใหม่)", "url": "https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-sena/", "image": "https://takuapa101.com/assets/wat-sena.webp", "description": "สถาปัตยกรรมอุโบสถมีองค์ประกอบจีนและสะท้อนวัฒนธรรมไทย-จีน", "address": {"@type": "PostalAddress", "streetAddress": "ตลาดเก่า ตำบลตะกั่วป่า", "addressLocality": "ตะกั่วป่า", "addressRegion": "พังงา", "addressCountry": "TH"}, "geo": {"@type": "GeoCoordinates", "latitude": 8.8302299579, "longitude": 98.3624509671}}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "สถานที่นี้มีประวัติความเป็นมาอย่างไร", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "วัดก่อตั้งใน พ.ศ. 2390 (ค.ศ. 1847) โดยพระยาเสนานุชิต (นุช ณ นคร) ผู้ปกครองเมืองตะกั่วป่า ทะเบียนวัดระบุวันตั้งวัดเป็น พ.ศ. 2390 และเป็นวัดราษฎร์มหานิกาย"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "มีจุดเด่นอะไรให้ชม", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "ชมอุโบสถขนาดใหญ่ที่ฐานข้อมูล ONEP ระบุว่าได้รับอิทธิพลจากสถาปัตยกรรมจีน แหล่งท่องเที่ยวระบุว่ามีพิพิธภัณฑ์จัดแสดงโบราณวัตถุ รวมถึงธรรมาสน์ไม้ที่มีอักษร จปร. แต่ควรตรวจสอบการเปิดชมหน้างาน ชมพื้นที่วัดที่ได้รับการขึ้นทะเบียนโบราณสถาน พ.ศ. 2539"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "เอกสารเทศบาลกล่าวถึงที่นี่อย่างไร", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "สถาปัตยกรรมอุโบสถมีองค์ประกอบจีนและสะท้อนวัฒนธรรมไทย-จีน"}}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-sena/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/wat-sena/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-sena/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">เรื่องเล่า</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">แผ่นพับ</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/wat-sena/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Temples & Pagodas</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Temples and pagodas</span><h1>Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan</h1><p class="english">Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">9 items verified</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="wat-sena">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">ดูทริปของคุณ</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/wat-sena.webp" alt="วัดเสนานุชรังสรรค์ (วัดใหม่)" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.8302299579, 98.3624509671 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.8302299579,98.3624509671" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#news">News and updates</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#getting_there">Getting there</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>Wat Mai Kamphaeng: The governor's temple in the mining town</h3><p class="era-years">Around 1847 (2390 BE)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Wat Senanucharangsan, or locally called “Wat Mai Kamphaeng”, emerged during the period when Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch na Nakhon) was the governor of Takua Pa. Sources from the Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre state that the temple was built after the town walls, government offices, and the governor's residence were completed. Thus, it is significant as both a Buddhist offering and a memorial to its builder.</p><p>The original area was a mound at the foot of Khao Chamonkhiri. Phraya Senanuchit had the area leveled, then built a brick and mortar wall about 2 meters high, with east and west gates, before constructing an ordination hall in the middle of the temple and two preaching halls on the sides. The name “Wat Mai Kamphaeng” (New Walled Temple) was therefore not just a name for a new temple, but reflected the town landscape taking shape along with administrative power and a community center.</p><p>In the era when Takua Pa was still an Andaman port town, connecting maritime trade and the tin economy, the temple's ordination hall was used as the site for the oath of allegiance ceremony. This shows that the temple directly connected religious rituals with the town's political order. Meanwhile, the ordination hall's style, which received Chinese influence, also aligned with the market district and Takua Pa's Hokkien Chinese community, but sources do not confirm that the craftsmen or patrons were directly Chinese.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) · Encyclopedia of Thai Culture</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning (ONEP)</a> · <a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">วารสารปาริชาต มหาวิทยาลัยทักษิณ (Parichart Journal, Thaksin University)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>From a ceremonial temple to old town heritage: Repairs, changes, and registration</h3><p class="era-years">1865·2023 (2408·2566 BE)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The temple continued to be the site for the oath of allegiance ceremony in the period before the 1932 (2475 BE) revolution. Meanwhile, the temple's history records Phra Khru Udom Panya (Nuam) as the abbot between 1865·1909 (2408·2452 BE), and disciples later built a stupa containing his relics around 2014 (2557 BE). This gives the temple layers of memory of both the town and the local monastic community.</p><p>The buildings do not entirely retain their original forms. The Encyclopedia of Thai Culture states that the ordination hall once had a three-tiered roof covered with scaled tiles, but it was repaired by reducing it to a single tier and replacing it with corrugated iron. Another set of temple registry data indicates that the current preaching hall was built in 1963 (2506 BE) and lists the date of receiving royal boundary consecration (Visungkhamasima) as 8 January 1898 (2440 BE). However, some year details in secondary sources have typing or scanning errors, so they are not used as evidence to confirm additional restoration years.</p><p>Krom Sinlapakon (Fine Arts Department) registered the temple's ancient monument status in 1996 (2539 BE), with the highlights being the wall around the temple and the large ordination hall with Chinese influence. Later, in the context of the Takua Pa old town revival, the temple is also home to the Takua Pa Community Museum, which collects utensils and equipment from the mining way of life. This makes the temple area function as both a religious site and a space recounting the town's memories after the mining era.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) · Encyclopedia of Thai Culture</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ONEP · Cultural environments for conservation</a> · <a href="https://books.classstart.org/thai-buddhist-temples/03/%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wat Senanucharangsan · Temple Database (ClassStart Books)</a> · <a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">วารสารปาริชาต มหาวิทยาลัยทักษิณ (Parichart Journal, Thaksin University)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>An active historical site, but access must be checked locally</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The latest accessible evidence confirms that Wat Senanucharangsan is still presented as a learning center and tourist attraction of Takua Pa old town. Tesaban Mueang Takua Pa (Takua Pa Town Municipality) has media introducing the temple, and in 2023 (2566 BE) published the candle procession and town circumambulation activities along with an offering ceremony to request permission to restore the ordination hall.</p><p>Inside the area, there are important elements to observe, namely the ordination hall, the brick wall around the temple, the stupa containing the relics of Phra Khru Udom Panya, and the Takua Pa Community Museum. Academic work indicates that the museum stores utensils and equipment from the mining era, allowing a visit to the temple to connect with the labor history, trade, and daily life of the town.</p><p>However, no official announcements have been found in 2026 (2569 BE) that confirm the name of the current caretaker, opening hours, areas open for visits, the condition of restoration results after the 2023 (2566 BE) ceremony, or regular temple activities. Therefore, the visiting status should be considered "must check before traveling" and it should not be concluded that the museum or ordination hall is open daily based solely on online information.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=76z9YMImUUk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tesaban Mueang Takua Pa (Takua Pa Town Municipality) · Introduction video and 2023 (2566 BE) activity announcements</a> · <a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">วารสารปาริชาต มหาวิทยาลัยทักษิณ (Parichart Journal, Thaksin University)</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ONEP · Cultural environments for conservation</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1847 (2390 BE)</p><h3>Construction of Wat Mai Kamphaeng</h3><p>Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon) built the temple after organizing the town wall and the Takua Pa office, constructing a wall and ubosot as the center for town religious ceremonies.</p><a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1865 (2408 BE)</p><h3>Phra Khru Udom Panya (Nuam) started as abbot</h3><p>Encyclopedia information specifies the tenure from 1865·1909 (2408·2452 BE) and names him as the first ecclesiastical provincial governor of Takua Pa.</p><a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">8 January 1898 (2440 BE)</p><h3>Received Wisungkhammasima</h3><p>Temple registry sources state the date of receiving Wisungkhammasima as January 8, 1898.</p><a href="https://books.classstart.org/thai-buddhist-temples/03/%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1932 (2475 BE)</p><h3>End of the water oath ceremony role in the old regime</h3><p>Temple history data states that the temple was used for the oath of allegiance ceremony until before the 1932 regime change.</p><a href="https://books.classstart.org/thai-buddhist-temples/03/%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">1963 (2506 BE)</p><h3>Construction of the current sermon hall</h3><p>The temple database indicates that the reinforced concrete sermon hall was built in 1963.</p><a href="https://books.classstart.org/thai-buddhist-temples/03/%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1996 (2539 BE)</p><h3>Registered as a historical site</h3><p>ONEP states that the temple was registered by the Fine Arts Department in 1996, emphasizing the value of the ubosot and Chinese architectural influences.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">2014 (2557 BE)</p><h3>Construction of the chedi for the ashes of Phra Khru Udom Panya (Nuam)</h3><p>Disciples built a chedi to the northeast of the ubosot to contain the ashes of Phra Khru Udom Panya (Nuam).</p><a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">26 July 2023 (2566 BE)</p><h3>Ubosot restoration permission ceremony</h3><p>The Takua Pa Town Municipality published news about the Buddhist Lent Day activity and the blessing ceremony to request permission to restore the ubosot, providing evidence of a restoration project during that time, but the success of the work is not verified.</p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=76z9YMImUUk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Chinese-influenced ordination hall</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>See the large ubosot which the ONEP database notes was influenced by Chinese architecture.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Museum and antiquities</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Tourism sources mention a museum displaying antiquities, including a wooden pulpit with the royal monogram of King Rama V, but opening hours should be checked on-site.</p><a href="https://live.phuketindex.com/tat-phangnga-6049.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Traces of a historical site</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Visit the temple grounds registered as a historical site in 1996 CE.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa old town landscape</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The temple is located in Takua Pa subdistrict and is one of the old town's key landmarks.</p><a href="https://tatmediaassets.com/file/1097577" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="news"><h2>News and updates</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2023-07-20 · Tesaban Mueang Takua Pa (Takua Pa Town Municipality)</p><h3>Takua Pa Town Municipality tours Wat Senanucharangsan and invites participation in the ubosot restoration permission ceremony</h3><p>The municipality's promotional video mentions the town's historic temple and notes the offering ceremony to request permission to restore the ordination hall on 26 July 2023 (2566 BE), alongside the candle procession and town circumambulation activities.</p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=76z9YMImUUk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The name "Wat Mai Kamphaeng" comes from the tall brick walls around the temple, not its original official name.</p><a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The ordination hall once had a three-tiered roof covered with wooden shingles before being repaired to a single tier with a corrugated iron roof.</p><a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The Takua Pa Community Museum inside the temple collects utensils and equipment reflecting the town's tin-mining era.</p><a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="getting_there"><h2>Getting there</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa Town</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Phuket</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Khao Lak</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Airport</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li><a href="/en/places/wat-boromthat/">Wat Boromthat Khiri Khet (the town's relic temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-khuha/">Wat Khuha Phimuk (the cave hill temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-pathum/">Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-kongkha/">Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-nikorn/">Wat Nikornwararam</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>The temple was founded in 1847 (2390 BE) by Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch na Nakhon), the governor of Takua Pa. The temple registry lists the founding year as 1847 (2390 BE) and as a Mahanikaya private temple.</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>See the large ordination hall which the ONEP database indicates was influenced by Chinese architecture. Tourist sources mention a museum displaying artifacts, including a wooden pulpit with the royal initials Jor Por Ror, but you should verify opening conditions on site. See the temple grounds that were registered as an ancient monument in 1996 (2539 BE).</p></details><details><summary>How the municipality document mentions this place</summary><p>The ordination hall architecture features Chinese elements and reflects Thai and Chinese culture.</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Samnakngan Nayobai Lae Phaen Sapphayakon Thammachat Lae Singwaetlom (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) (ONEP) · Art and Cultural Sites to be Conserved</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Phang Nga Province temple registration report</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.50amfh.org/userfiles/file/News/Thai%20temple%20name%20list/south/Phangnga.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Phuketindex · Phang Nga’s Eight Shrines and Eight Temples</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://live.phuketindex.com/tat-phangnga-6049.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">TAT Media Assets · Wat Sena Nucharangsan</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://tatmediaassets.com/file/1097577" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) · Encyclopedia of Thai Culture</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ONEP · Cultural environments for conservation</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">วารสารปาริชาต มหาวิทยาลัยทักษิณ (Parichart Journal, Thaksin University)</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Thetsaban Mueang Takua Pa (Takua Pa Town Municipality) · Promotional video for activities and ordination hall restoration</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=76z9YMImUUk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">TAT Media Assets · Image of Wat Senanut Rangsan</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://tatmediaassets.com/file/1097561" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>A verified postcode for the temple location could not be found directly from the sources.</li><li>Verified opening hours could not be found.</li><li>Verified admission fees could not be found.</li><li>Verified official phone numbers, Facebook pages, websites, or LINE accounts could not be found.</li><li>Verified information on parking, restrooms, wheelchair access, and dress codes could not be found.</li><li>Verified distances or routes from Takua Pa town centre, Phuket, Khao Lak, and the airport could not be found.</li><li>Evidence confirming the temple's direct role in the tin-mining era could not be found.</li><li>Temple-specific festivals or recommended visiting periods could not be found.</li><li>Nearby places confirmed to be within walking distance are not verified.</li><li>Evidence confirming the current caretaker or abbot in 2026 CE has not been found.</li><li>Opening hours, admission fees, and official announcements stating the ubosot or museum is open daily have not been found.</li><li>Not yet found opening · closing hours, admission fees, and official announcements stating that the ordination hall or museum is open to visitors every day.</li><li>Specific evidence of how the 2004 CE tsunami damaged the temple has not been found.</li><li>Direct evidence of the temple being abandoned or changing hands has not been found.</li><li>News from the past five years confirming activities or projects outside of the 2023 CE reports has not been found.</li><li>No news in the past 5 years has been found to confirm other activities or projects aside from the 2023 (B.E. 2566) news/media.</li><li>Image from TAT Media Assets with unknown rights. Permission must be requested before use.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\stories\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Stories of Takua Pa: History, Tin and the River · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="Long reads on how Takua Pa grew: the river that moved the town, buildings paid for with tin money, Peranakan food, and the old town waking up again."><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Stories of Takua Pa: History, Tin and the River"><meta property="og:description" content="Long reads on how Takua Pa grew: the river that moved the town, buildings paid for with tin money, Peranakan food, and the old town waking up again."><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Takua Pa Stories", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/"}]}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "ItemList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "url": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "url": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/architecture/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "url": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/water-trade/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 4, "url": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/food-people/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 5, "url": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">เรื่องเล่า</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">แผ่นพับ</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="stories-index-v2"><div class="intro"><a class="eyebrow" href="/en/">Takua Pa 101 / Old Town Guide</a><h1>Takua Pa Stories</h1><p class="lead">Four perspectives for reading the town through its sources · Getting to know the town, old houses and architecture, waterways and trade, people and flavors</p></div><div class="story-grid-v2"><article class="story-card-v2 story-card-v2-lead"><div class="card-art-v2"><img src="/assets/riverwalk.webp" alt="" loading="lazy"></div><div class="card-content-v2"><span class="chapter">Know the city</span><h2><a href="/en/stories/city/">Takua Pa: The Town That Moved with the River</a></h2><p>From an ancient settlement on the trans-peninsular route to a market and mining town · Verified facts and questions waiting to be answered.</p><div class="card-meta-v2"><span class="story-mins">6 minutes</span><span class="card-go" aria-hidden="true">Open this story ↗</span></div></div></article><article class="story-card-v2"><div class="card-art-v2"><img src="/assets/culture-street.webp" alt="" loading="lazy"></div><div class="card-content-v2"><span class="chapter">Old houses and architecture</span><h2><a href="/en/stories/architecture/">Buildings built with tin money and dredger steel · Architecture that remembers the town</a></h2><p>Read Takua Pa through the shophouses on Culture Street, Boon Sung Iron Bridge, the governor's residence, and Tao Ming School · The transition embedded in brick, mortar, and steel structures</p><div class="card-meta-v2"><span class="story-mins">6 minutes</span><span class="card-go" aria-hidden="true">Open this story ↗</span></div></div></article><article class="story-card-v2"><div class="card-art-v2"><img src="/assets/iron-bridge.webp" alt="" loading="lazy"></div><div class="card-content-v2"><span class="chapter">Waterways and trade</span><h2><a href="/en/stories/water-trade/">From Thung Tuk to the Takua Pa River: The Day the Waterway Built the Town</a></h2><p>The river is not just a backdrop. It is a route, a connector of people, and the determiner of the town's fate, which had to relocate due to sedimentation.</p><div class="card-meta-v2"><span class="story-mins">7 minutes</span><span class="card-go" aria-hidden="true">Open this story ↗</span></div></div></article><article class="story-card-v2"><div class="card-art-v2"><img src="/assets/food-center.webp" alt="" loading="lazy"></div><div class="card-content-v2"><span class="chapter">People and flavors</span><h2><a href="/en/stories/food-people/">Flavours of the Baba Community and the Old Market</a></h2><p>Food serves as both household memories and evidence of cultural exchange · Read Takua Pa through the Baba cuisine, double-layered Tao Sor, and the Culture Street</p><div class="card-meta-v2"><span class="story-mins">7 minutes</span><span class="card-go" aria-hidden="true">Open this story ↗</span></div></div></article><article class="story-card-v2"><div class="card-art-v2"><img src="/assets/tao-ming.webp" alt="" loading="lazy"></div><div class="card-content-v2"><span class="chapter">City topics</span><h2><a href="/en/stories/kuapapoh/">Kuapapoh: The Day Old Houses Stand Up to Tell Their Stories</a></h2><p>A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover</p><div class="card-meta-v2"><span class="story-mins">6 minutes</span><span class="card-go" aria-hidden="true">Open this story ↗</span></div></div></article></div><aside class="source"><h2>How to read this page</h2><p>This article was compiled from official documents and academic-institution databases, with the evidence level identified for each topic · Check shop details, opening hours, and schedules with providers before travelling</p></aside></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\stories\kuapapoh\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Houses Reopened · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Houses Reopened"><meta property="og:description" content="A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "หน้าแรก", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "กั่วป่าโพ้ บ้านเก่าตะกั่วป่าที่กลับมามีชีวิต", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">เรื่องเล่า</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">แผ่นพับ</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/kuapapoh/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>City topics</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · Town Topics</span><h1>Kuapapoh: The Day Old Houses Stand Up to Tell Their Stories</h1><p class="lead">A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover</p><p class="story-meta">Updated on 2026-09-07 · 6-minute read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" alt="กั่วป่าโพ้ · แบรนด์ของโครงการเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Kuapapoh, the identity of the old town project</span><span class="credit">โครงการกั่วป่าโพ้ (The Kuapapoh project)</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>Imagine strolling along Sri Takua Pa Road, a street lined with old shophouses that have stood the test of time. Many of these buildings have their wooden doors quietly shut, sleeping away alongside the long-gone golden age of tin mining. Underneath that quietness, however, lie countless memories of the town, its people, and its trade, waiting to be told.</p><p>In recent years, a creative space project called 'Kuapapoh Ji Show Mueang' has stepped in to open the doors of these once-abandoned old houses and bring them back to life. They did not just repaint or repair the roofs, but are bringing the stories of Takua Pa back on display for the newer generations to see.</p><p>This movement reflects a town trying to stand up and tell its own story through the perspectives of locals, artists, and community collaboration. However, in reading these changes, we must still distinguish between newly created narratives for tourism and historical facts that still need careful verification.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">The Once Sleeping Spaces Return to the Town</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/shophouse-109.webp" alt="ตึกแถวเลขที่ 109 บนถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Shophouse number 109 on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><p>We often remember Takua Pa through its prosperous tin mining era. When that industry declined, many buildings were left behind. One of them is house number 78, formerly a mine owner's residence and a tin mining company office. Today, this once-empty space has been transformed into the 'Kuapa Mueang Museum', exhibiting mining equipment, old maps, and important documents for visitors to experience the traces of the past.</p><p>Not far away on the same route is house number 109, the building of the Kang Tang Yui Kuan or Wui Kun shrine, an association of the Hakka Chinese. The second floor of the building still houses the deity Tham Kung Ya for people to pay their respects. But the ground floor space, which used to be empty and unused, has been turned into the 'Kuapapoh Gallery' to host exhibitions and showcase the wisdom of the Takua Pa old town residents.</p><p>Another significant building that the project has dusted off is building number 305, or 'Tao Ming School' (the town's first Chinese school), which was an educational center during the prosperous mining era. The team came up with the idea to revive this space under the name 'Tao Ming: The Spark of Learning', using it as a venue to exhibit knowledge on architecture, culture, and traditions related to Takua Pa.</p><p>The effort to bring these three spaces back into use is an initiative filled with the project owners' determination to add economic value and create worth for the town. We must collectively understand, however, that this is an explanation and operation at the community project level, not an official declaration of historical site status or historical endorsement from government agencies.</p></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Working with community narratives is about balancing beautiful memories with facts that must be verified.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">Kuapapoh: A Moniker Carried by the River</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/tao-ming-school.jpg" alt="อาคารโรงเรียนเต้าหมิง ถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Tao Ming School seen from the front, on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><p>As soon as you hear the name 'Kuapapoh', the first question that usually follows is where this word comes from. The project's archival information explains that this was the term Hokkien Chinese used in the past to call Takua Pa town, dividing the translation into two parts: 'Kuapa' meaning Takua Pa, and 'Poh' meaning market or port.</p><p>When combined, the word conveys 'Takua Pa port market', reflecting the past image of Chinese people sailing across the sea to trade and mine tin, using the pier as a center for exchanging both goods and culture. In addition, in the community's belief, the word Kuapapoh is also embedded in the rituals performed at various shrines during the vegetarian festival, becoming a word with deep meaning in the hearts of the townspeople.</p><p>While this is a beautiful story that connects well with the community's roots, in academic circles, the origin of the Takua Pa town name is still debated with several suggested, not settled hypotheses. Some refer to the ancient name Takola, while others base it on the words takua (lead) and pa (forest).</p><p>Therefore, the project's explanation is considered a record of memory and interpretation through the context of the Hokkien Chinese community, rather than a single, settled historical academic conclusion. This is the reason we must read this story with an open mind and caution.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">not settled academically</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">Peranakan Charm Through the Brush Tip</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/tao-ming.webp" alt="โรงเรียนเต้าหมิง" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Tao Ming School</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The liveliness of the old town district reappeared concretely in September 2026 (2569 BE) when 78 Studio on Takua Pa Culture Street opened its space to host a solo art exhibition titled 'Peranakan Charm · Still Becoming'.</p><p>The artist behind these works is Tinnaphop Ngansathir, or Chang Pong. He is a native of Takua Pa old market, growing up with cartoon drawings, superheroes, and Chinese deities familiar in the local culture. After accumulating artistic experience for a long time, he chose to use his brush tip to convey the stories of the town he is attached to.</p><p>The artworks in the exhibition focus on extracting Peranakan colors, patterns, and clothing for display, serving as a bridge connecting the glorious past with people in the present era. Each painting is not just visually beautiful, but an emotional record of the artist towards the town.</p><p>Even though the project documents clearly record the exhibition hosting and the opening of space for artists to express themselves, what we still do not see in the reports are the number of attendees or a formal economic impact assessment. These activities thus serve as cultural ripples rather than official government statistics.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">Viewing the Town Through the Lens of Six Heritages</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" alt="ลายกระเบื้องเปอรานากันที่ยังเห็นได้ในเมืองเก่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Peranakan tile patterns still visible in the old town</span><span class="credit">โครงการกั่วป่าโพ้ (The Kuapapoh project)</span></figcaption></figure><p>To help visitors understand the identity of Takua Pa old town more deeply, the project did not stop at organizing exhibitions. They also compiled and presented the '6 Or' concept to use as a framework for viewing the town's heritage hidden in daily life.</p><p>This concept starts with 'Aphon' (Apparel) or local clothing, whether it is lace fabrics, batik sarongs, and various accessories. Followed by 'Asai' (Abode) which covers the way of life, trading, and living in accordance with the times. As for 'Ahan' (Food), it is indispensable. It is the traditional flavors passed down from generation to generation, and the old restaurants that still keep their stoves on to this day.</p><p>In the dimension of the mind, the town is driven by 'Arom' (Emotion) expressed through art, music, and the sound of drums in the vegetarian festival. Meanwhile, 'Aram' (Temples and Shrines) is the center of faith, including temples, shrines, and Yui Kuan scattered around the old town district. And finally, 'Akhan' (Architecture), the structures that stand as witnesses to change.</p><p>All these concepts were also further developed in the design of souvenirs, such as the Poh Shop project's t-shirts. The revenue from the sales is brought back to fund the restoration and drive the town's activities forward, truly circulating value back to the community.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">What the Town Tells and What We Must Verify</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/culture-street.webp" alt="ถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Kuapapoh project allows us to see the determination of the new generation and locals who want to preserve and build upon the stories of Takua Pa. They created creative spaces, brought in art as a medium, and turned once-closed houses into new travel destinations.</p><p>What is recorded on the project's website and documents serves as a contemporary archive that the community chose to tell about itself. It is a memory full of life and the pride of the residents.</p><p>At the same time, however, reading these stories also calls for us to maintain a balance between appreciation and historical facts. Many parts of the information are explanations that have not been endorsed by government agencies and cannot replace calendars or academic documents.</p><p>This is the challenge and fun of walking around Takua Pa old town today. We have the task of listening to those beautiful stories while simultaneously asking questions and finding answers from reliable sources, so that our journey does not end with just looking at pictures, but truly understanding the town in all dimensions.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Tao Ming School</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="tao-ming">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="culture-street">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Takuapa Riverwalk</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="riverwalk">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The number of attendees or the official economic impact generated by the project</li><li>The legal status or endorsement from government agencies of the three houses</li><li>The clear and settled origin of the Takua Pa town name in history</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Kuapapoh · Project Archives</cite> · Kuapapoh Project · content.json · origin, events, openings sections · <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">Defining houses number 78, 109, and 305 as creative spaces</div><div class="ev-status qualified">QUALIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">This is an explanation from the project owner, not a declaration of historical site or space status from a government agency. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The origin of the word Kuapapoh translates to Takua Pa port market</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">The origin of the Takua Pa town name in historical work still has several suggested, not settled hypotheses, thus the project's explanation should not be used as a settled academic conclusion. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The hosting of the Peranakan Charm exhibition by Tinnaphop Ngansathir in September 2026 (2569 BE)</div><div class="ev-status qualified">QUALIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">Referenced from project data, but not a retrospective official calendar confirming attendee numbers or economic impact. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">The Once Sleeping Spaces Return to the Town</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">Kuapapoh: A Moniker Carried by the River</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">Peranakan Charm Through the Brush Tip</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">Viewing the Town Through the Lens of Six Heritages</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">What the Town Tells and What We Must Verify</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div><section class="research-section" id="project-gallery"><h2>Images from Kuapapoh Project Archive</h2><div data-gallery><div class="gallery-tabs" hidden><button type="button" data-tab="today">Present photos</button><button type="button" data-tab="archive">Historical photos</button></div><div id="gallery-today" data-panel="today"><h3>Present photos</h3><div class="photo-grid"><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · กั่วป่าโพ้ แบรนด์เมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" alt="กั่วป่าโพ้ แบรนด์เมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Town Brand<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · ลายกระเบื้องเปอรานากันเมืองเก่า"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" alt="ลายกระเบื้องเปอรานากันเมืองเก่า" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Old Town Peranakan Tile Patterns<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure></div></div><div id="gallery-archive" data-panel="archive"><h3>Historical photos</h3><div class="photo-grid"><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-history.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · แผ่นเล่าประวัติกั่วป่าโพ้"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-history.jpg" alt="แผ่นเล่าประวัติกั่วป่าโพ้" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Kuapapoh History Board<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-wuikun.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · หวุ่ยกุ่น"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-wuikun.jpg" alt="หวุ่ยกุ่น" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Wui Kun<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-1.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 1"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-1.jpg" alt="เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 1" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Takua Pa Old Document 1<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-2.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-2.jpg" alt="เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 2" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Takua Pa Old Document 2<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure></div></div></div></section></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\stories\architecture\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Old Buildings of Takua Pa and Tin Money · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="Read Takua Pa through the shophouses on Culture Street, Boon Sung Iron Bridge, the governor&#x27;s residence, and Tao Ming School · The transition embedded in bric"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Old Buildings of Takua Pa and Tin Money"><meta property="og:description" content="Read Takua Pa through the shophouses on Culture Street, Boon Sung Iron Bridge, the governor&#x27;s residence, and Tao Ming School · The transition embedded in bric"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/architecture/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/architecture/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "หน้าแรก", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "ตึกเก่าตะกั่วป่า สถาปัตยกรรมยุคเหมืองดีบุก", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/architecture/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/architecture/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/architecture/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/architecture/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">เรื่องเล่า</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">แผ่นพับ</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/architecture/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>Old houses and architecture</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa stories · Old houses and architecture</span><h1>Buildings built with tin money and dredger steel · Architecture that remembers the town</h1><p class="lead">Read Takua Pa through the shophouses on Culture Street, Boon Sung Iron Bridge, the governor's residence, and Tao Ming School · The transition embedded in brick, mortar, and steel structures</p><p class="story-meta">Updated on 2026-09-07 · 6-minute read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/tao-ming-school.jpg" alt="อาคารโรงเรียนเต้าหมิง ถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Tao Ming School seen from the front, on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>If you walk through the Thanon Udom Thara and Thanon Si Takua Pa areas today, the first sight is rows of Sino-Portuguese shophouses. The front walkway has a roof cover known as &#x27;Ngo Ka Ki&#x27; which provides shade and rain protection for pedestrians. It feels like stepping back in time to a well-preserved past.</p><p>But these buildings are not just beautiful backdrops for photography. They are a massive archive recording the changes of Takua Pa in the form of brick, mortar, and steel structures. Each piece of architecture tells the story of a town that grew and prospered through tin mining, water trade, and the blending of people from diverse cultures.</p><p>To deeply understand Takua Pa's architecture, we must look beyond its external beauty. We need to distinguish between what is &#x27;newly built&#x27; and what is &#x27;repaired&#x27;, and understand the layers of history hidden within the governor's residence walls, the miners' iron bridge, and the school built from merchants' donations.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">The governor's residence and the wall that tells of conflict</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/culture-street.webp" alt="ถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>Back in the era when Takua Pa was growing from the tin mining economy and coastal trade, wealth came with conflicts of interest. The wall of the Takua Pa governor's residence was built to act like a camp wall, protecting against danger and serving as a refuge for townspeople. Scholars suggest, though not settled, that this wall was built during the time when Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon) was the governor between 1840 (2383 BE) and 1881.</p><p>The wall was constructed with plaster mixed with gravel and sand, layered with a base wider than the wall itself for stability. The part that remains today is about 5 meters high. The inner area was once divided into a front palace, a main hall, and a rear palace, reflecting the systematic space allocation of the town's rulers in that era.</p><p>Important evidence confirming the prosperity of trade in that era comes from the archaeological excavation by the Fine Arts Department in 1999, which found Qing dynasty Chinese ceramics, European ceramics, glassware, and building decorative parts. These items confirm that this governor's residence was once connected to a global trade network.</p><p>When the city center moved to Ko Kho Khao and Ban Yan Yao, the former governor's residence lost its administrative role. However, today this area has become an open archaeological site that the community uses for cultural events, such as the &#x27;From Takola to Takua Pa&#x27; festival, bringing the old walls back to life as the city's stage.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The year the wall construction began</span></div></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Architecture is not just a beautiful backdrop. It is an archive recording the changes of Takua Pa in the form of brick, mortar, and steel structures.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">The district chief's house in the transition era</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/governor-wall.webp" alt="กำแพงจวนเจ้าเมืองตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Governor’s Residence Wall</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The transition from the governor system to the district system was recorded in the architecture of &#x27;Ban Khun In&#x27;. According to local evidence, this house was built around 1917 (B.E. 2460) by Chinese craftsmen to be the residence of Lieutenant Khun Intharakhiri (Choi na Nakhon), a descendant of the governor lineage who later became the Takua Pa district chief.</p><p>The building is a two-story brick and masonry structure in an applied Sino-Portuguese style, reflecting the architectural influence from port cities like Penang and Singapore that spread through maritime trade. This house therefore not only displays the status of its owner but also serves as evidence showing Takua Pa's connection to the outside world.</p><p>In reading history from architecture, we must be careful to distinguish between the year of construction and the year of repair. A report from the Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning indicates that the Na Nakhon family repaired this house in 1981, trying to preserve the original style but changing the roofing material to corrugated tiles.</p><p>Knowing this repair information helps us see Ban Khun In not just as a static past, but as a living structure that has been maintained and improved by its residents over many decades.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The year Ban Khun In was built (1917)</span></div></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>From an industrial structure, the Boon Sung Iron Bridge has changed its role to a community thoroughfare and has become a major landmark attracting people to visit the traces of the mining town.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">Educational funds from mining and merchant networks</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/iron-bridge.webp" alt="สะพานเหล็กบุญสูง" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Boon Soong Iron Bridge</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The wealth from tin mining was not only spent on building private residences, but also led to the creation of community institutions. The &#x27;Tao Ming School&#x27; building, constructed in 1922 (B.E. 2465), is a clear example of community collaboration.</p><p>Funds for constructing this school came from fundraising among wealthy miners and Chinese merchants from the Old Town market, Yan Yao, Ranong, and Phuket. This shows the strong geographical and economic network of the Chinese community in this region.</p><p>The school building was built by Chinese craftsmen in an applied Sino-Portuguese style and operated as a primary school for a long time. It served as a center for passing on knowledge, language, and culture to generations of Thai-Chinese children in Takua Pa.</p><p>Even though the school has ceased its teaching operations today, the building is still maintained by the Takua Pa Tao Ming School Foundation, standing as a memorial to the community's commitment to education during the town's golden era.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">From a tin dredger to a workers' bridge</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/khun-in.webp" alt="บ้านขุนอินทร์" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Khun In House</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>While residences and schools reflect the wealth of merchants, the &#x27;Saphan Lek Bun Sung&#x27; (Bun Sung Iron Bridge) tells the story of the industrial era and the working class. This bridge was built in 1968 (B.E. 2511) and is a direct product of the city's tin mining industry.</p><p>Before the bridge existed, miners and villagers had to rely on rowboats to cross the Takua Pa Canal, which is over 40 meters wide, making it extremely difficult during the flood season. Although a wooden bridge for pedestrians and bicycles had been built, as the industry expanded, the demand for a stronger structure increased.</p><p>The Juti Boon Sung Dredging Company initiated the construction of this bridge by assembling the steel frames from a decommissioned dredger to form the Khok Khanun iron bridge, which is over 200 meters long. This bridge became an important shortcut connecting the Old Town market area to the mining area, facilitating labor and transportation.</p><p>Although the sound of mining machinery has long gone silent today, the iron bridge remains. It has changed its role from an industrial structure to a community thoroughfare and has become a major landmark that attracts people to visit the traces of the Takua Pa mining town.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">Culture Street and shophouses brought back to life</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/shophouse-78.webp" alt="ตึกแถวเลขที่ 78 บนถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Shophouse number 78 on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><p>The shophouses along both sides of Udom Tara and Sri Takua Pa streets are the town's most prominent architectural heritage. These buildings were built to align with the commercial lifestyle, featuring ground-floor spaces for business and connected Ngo Ka Ki walkways. These buildings were influenced by Straits colonial architecture, reflecting the trade networks with Penang and Singapore.</p><p>After the tin mining industry declined and the town's commercial center moved away, this Old Town market area faced quietness. Many buildings were left to decay over time, as if the golden era of this street had passed.</p><p>However, the surviving architecture has become an important asset in revitalizing this area. Around 2007 (B.E. 2550), the &#x27;Takua Pa Old Town Reminiscing&#x27; project was initiated, piloting the opening of the street space as a cultural walking street before developing into the current Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street.</p><p>Every Sunday, this street is closed to traffic and opens as a bustling market. The old Sino-Portuguese buildings return to their role as a backdrop for community activities, including the selling of local food, crafts, and performances, proving that past architecture can find new life and roles in the present day.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-disputed">The year the walking street project started</span></div></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="culture-street">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Governor’s Residence Wall</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="governor-wall">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Boon Soong Iron Bridge</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="iron-bridge">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Khun In House</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="khun-in">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Tao Ming School</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="tao-ming">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><aside class="story-outro"><p>อาคารในบทความนี้ส่วนใหญ่ดูได้เฉพาะภายนอก · บ้านไม้สักหลังหนึ่งในย่านตลาดเก่าเปิดเป็นคาเฟ่ จึงเป็นหนึ่งในไม่กี่หลังที่เดินเข้าไปดูโครงสร้างไม้ ฝ้า และช่องแสงข้างในได้จริง</p><a class="text-link" href="https://siwaracafe.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">บ้านไม้สักที่เข้าไปข้างในได้ ↗</a></aside><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The exact years of construction for Ban Khun In and the Takua Pa governor's residence wall</li><li>Documentary information on the later structural repair and restoration of the Boon Sung Iron Bridge</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Old town boundary demarcation project · Takua Pa Old Town</cite> · Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning (ONEP) · PDF pages 55 to 58 · <a href="https://nced.onep.go.th/opendata/2568/oldtown36_announce/takuapa.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Tao Ming School building</cite> · สผ. (ONEP) Cultural Art Environment Database · Significance/Characteristics section · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4420" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Khun In building</cite> · สผ. (ONEP) Cultural Art Environment Database · Significance/Characteristics section · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4423" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Governor’s Residence Wall</cite> · Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre (public organisation) · Archaeological highlights · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Thanon Watthanatham Mueang Kao Takua Pa</cite> · Department of Internal Trade · Market records · <a href="https://www.dit.go.th/th/market/2383/ภาคใต/พ-งงา/ถนนว-ฒนธรรมเม-องเก-าตะก-วป-า/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">The year Ban Khun In was built</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">Local information states it was built in 1917, but the ONEP report and official databases do not specify a clear construction year. Therefore, primary evidence is needed for confirmation. <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4423" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The year Culture Street started</div><div class="ev-status disputed">DISPUTED</div><div class="ev-explain">The Department of Internal Trade's market registry states the founding or promotion date as 17 December 2017, but news sources and research indicate activities began since 2007. It may therefore be different milestones between event organization and registration. <a href="https://www.dit.go.th/th/market/2383/ภาคใต/พ-งงา/ถนนว-ฒนธรรมเม-องเก-าตะก-วป-า/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The year the governor's residence wall was built</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">Scholars suggest, though not settled, that it was built during the time of Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch) between 1840 and 1881, but no evidence specifying the exact year construction began has yet been found. <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">The governor's residence and the wall that tells of conflict</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">The district chief's house in the transition era</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">Educational funds from mining and merchant networks</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">From a tin dredger to a workers' bridge</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">Culture Street and shophouses brought back to life</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
