rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/museum/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">เรื่องเล่า</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">แผ่นพับ</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/museum/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Museums</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Museum</span><h1>Takua Pa Community Museum</h1><p class="english">Takua Pa Community Museum</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">8 items verified</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="museum">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">ดูทริปของคุณ</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/museum.webp" alt="พิพิธภัณฑ์เสนานุชรังสรรค์ชุมชนตะกั่วป่า" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.8302299579, 98.3624509671 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.8302299579,98.3624509671" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd>Modest attire is recommended when entering the temple grounds. <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9610000016873" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a><a href="https://www.gplace.com/960255" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#news">News and updates</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>Wat Mai Kamphaeng in a mining and port town</h3><p class="era-years">Since 1847 (B.E. 2390)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>This place began at Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan, formerly known by local residents as “Wat Mai Kamphaeng.” The temple register records its establishment in B.E. 2390 (1847), while local sources say it was built during the reign of King Rama III by Phraya Sena Nuchit (Nuch), governor of Takuapa.</p><p>At that time, Takuapa was an Andaman coastal port linking trade and tin resources. Various groups, especially workers and Chinese merchants, settled here, forming a market district and Chinese culture. The temple's Chinese-influenced ordination hall is therefore evidence of a multicultural town. For the museum seen today, no publicly available evidence identifies the year it was established or its direct founder.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.50amfh.org/userfiles/file/News/Thai%20temple%20name%20list/south/Phangnga.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phang Nga Province temple registration report</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cultural environments for conservation (ONEP)</a> · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/951" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Takua Pa Town</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>From a temple and community to a museum of memories</h3><p class="era-years">No establishment year for the museum has been found; evidence shows its use in the old-town context before 2018 (B.E. 2561).</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Over time, Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan has remained a religious site and community space. Meanwhile, Takuapa changed from a centre of tin mining and trade into a historic town preserving its heritage. Academic sources describe Takuapa Community Museum as a two-storey building displaying household items, equipment, and rare objects reflecting local life, including traces of the mining era.</p><p>The temple's ordination hall is registered as an ancient monument by the Fine Arts Department, but the sources verifiable this time do not clearly state the registration year. No evidence has been found that the museum building was abandoned, underwent major restoration, or was directly affected by the 2004 tsunami (B.E. 2547), so no conclusion is made beyond the evidence.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">วารสารปาริชาต มหาวิทยาลัยทักษิณ (Parichart Journal, Thaksin University)</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cultural environments for conservation (ONEP)</a> · <a href="https://www.andamannlttrang.com/%E0%B8%82%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B9%E0%B8%A5/%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%B8%E0%B9%82%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%96%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สารสนเทศท้องถิ่นภาคใต้ฝั่งอันดามัน (Andaman Southern Thai Local Information)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>A small museum on the old town route</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Contemporary sources call this place both “Takuapa Community Museum” and “Takuapa House Museum.” It is presented in the context of the old-town cultural street, displaying photographs, artefacts, and community stories alongside Sino-Portuguese buildings and old temples. Its role is therefore more that of a place for learning about the town's memories than a large museum with full visitor services.</p><p>However, no official announcement has been found confirming opening hours, admission fees, the direct custodian, the building's latest condition, or a museum-specific restoration project in 2026 (B.E. 2569). Old-town and cultural-street activities continue at municipal and community levels, but this should not be interpreted as evidence that the museum opens every day.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.dit.go.th/th/market/2383/%E0%B8%A0%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%83%E0%B8%95/%E0%B8%9E-%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%B2/%E0%B8%96%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%A7-%E0%B8%92%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A1%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%A1-%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%87%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81-%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81-%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B-%E0%B8%B2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">กรมการค้าภายใน กระทรวงพาณิชย์ (Department of Internal Trade, Ministry of Commerce)</a> · <a href="https://www.phangngapao.go.th/travel/detail/89" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phang Nga Province: Takuapa Old Town</a> · <a href="https://radiothailand.prd.go.th/th/content/category/detail/id/9/iid/481052" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สถานีวิทยุกระจายเสียงแห่งประเทศไทย (Radio Thailand)</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1847 (2390 BE)</p><h3>Establishment of Wat Sena Nucharangsan</h3><p>Temple records state it was established in 1847 (2390 BE); local sources identify Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch) as the founder.</p><a href="https://www.50amfh.org/userfiles/file/News/Thai%20temple%20name%20list/south/Phangnga.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">2026 (2569 BE)</p><h3>Part of old town tourism context</h3><p>Takua Pa Community Museum is mentioned as part of the old town cultural street, but no specific opening hours are announced.</p><a href="https://www.dit.go.th/th/market/2383/%E0%B8%A0%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%83%E0%B8%95/%E0%B8%9E-%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%B2/%E0%B8%96%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%A7-%E0%B8%92%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A1%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%87%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81-%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81-%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B-%E0%B8%B2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Ordination hall registered as an ancient monument</h3><p>The Fine Arts Department registered the ordination hall, but the reviewed sources do not specify the year.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Community museum established in a two-story building</h3><p>Academic sources describe the building and exhibits, but the establishment year and founder are not found.</p><a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Two-storey museum building</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The community museum is located within Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan.</p><a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Mining-era tools and utensils</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>It houses items from the mining era and rare objects reflecting local life.</p><a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Chinese-influenced ordination hall</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The large ordination hall features designs influenced by Chinese architecture.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa old town context</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Situated in an old town community featuring Sino-Portuguese buildings and mining heritage.</p><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/951" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="news"><h2>News and updates</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2026-03-01 · Radio Thailand</p><h3>Phang Nga hosts the 12th 'From Takola to Takua Pa' event</h3><p>The event promotes Takua Pa old town tourism, linking to local lifestyle learning and community economy, but the news does not directly confirm activities inside the museum.</p><a href="https://radiothailand.prd.go.th/th/content/category/detail/id/9/iid/481052" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2024-08-28 · Government Public Relations Department</p><h3>Tracing the legend of Takola to the charm of Takua Pa old town</h3><p>Phang Nga province and the municipality promote old town tourism and stimulate the community economy. No specific mention of museum restoration.</p><a href="https://www.prd.go.th/th/content/category/detail/id/2040/iid/319567" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan was formerly known as "Wat Mai Kamphaeng", and its ordination hall was influenced by Chinese architecture.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The Takua Pa Community Museum is a two-storey building that houses utensils from the tin-mining era.</p><a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li>Sin Chai Tung Shrine <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://mapcarta.com/N7619565714" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/kue-chai/">Kue Chai Tung Shrine</a> <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></li><li><a href="/en/places/khun-in/">Khun In House</a> <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></li><li><a href="/en/places/governor-wall/">Governor’s Residence Wall</a> <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>How to dress</summary><p>Modest attire is recommended when entering the temple grounds.</p></details><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>Takuapa Community Museum is a two-storey building inside Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan. It collects household items and equipment reflecting life in Takuapa, including objects from the mining era. Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan was built by Phraya Sena Nuchit (Nuch), and its ordination hall shows Chinese architectural influence.</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>The community museum is inside Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan. It preserves mining-era objects and rare items reflecting local life. The large ordination hall is influenced by Chinese architecture.</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Cultural environments for conservation (ONEP)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">วารสารปาริชาต มหาวิทยาลัยทักษิณ (Parichart Journal, Thaksin University)</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Community database, Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/951" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Mapcarta / OpenStreetMap</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://mapcarta.com/N7619565714" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Phang Nga Province temple registration report</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.50amfh.org/userfiles/file/News/Thai%20temple%20name%20list/south/Phangnga.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Department of Archaeology, Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">MGR Online</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9610000016873" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Takua Pa Town</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/951" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">สารสนเทศท้องถิ่นภาคใต้ฝั่งอันดามัน (Andaman Southern Thai Local Information)</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.andamannlttrang.com/%E0%B8%82%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B9%E0%B8%A5/%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%B8%E0%B9%82%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%96%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">กรมการค้าภายใน กระทรวงพาณิชย์ (Department of Internal Trade, Ministry of Commerce)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.dit.go.th/th/market/2383/%E0%B8%A0%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%83%E0%B8%95/%E0%B8%9E-%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%B2/%E0%B8%96%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%A7-%E0%B8%92%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A1%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%87%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">สถานีวิทยุกระจายเสียงแห่งประเทศไทย (Radio Thailand)</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://radiothailand.prd.go.th/th/content/category/detail/id/9/iid/481052" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>Direct opening hours for the museum could not be found.</li><li>Verified admission fees for the museum could not be found.</li><li>Direct phone numbers, Facebook pages, websites, or LINE accounts for the museum could not be found.</li><li>Distances and routes from Takua Pa town centre, Phuket, Khao Lak, or the airport could not be found.</li><li>Verified information on the establishment year, founder, and registration details of the museum building itself could not be found.</li><li>Verified information on parking, restrooms, or wheelchair accessibility could not be found.</li><li>A verified duration for visits could not be found.</li><li>Details regarding specific museum activities in 2026 CE have not been found.</li><li>Evidence confirming the museum was abandoned, changed hands, or directly affected by the 2004 CE tsunami has not been found.</li><li>Official image links with verified usage rights could not be found.</li><li>No direct custodian, opening hours, admission fees, or announcement confirming that it remains open to visitors in 2026 (B.E. 2569) has been found.</li><li>No news from the past five years mentioning restoration or a museum-specific project has been found.</li><li>No verified image URL has been found that can be clearly identified as showing the museum itself, so photos_archive and photos_current are left as empty arrays.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\stories\food-people\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Peranakan Food in Takua Pa Old Town · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="Food serves as both household memories and evidence of cultural exchange · Read Takua Pa through the Baba cuisine, double-layered Tao Sor, and the Culture Str"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Peranakan Food in Takua Pa Old Town"><meta property="og:description" content="Food serves as both household memories and evidence of cultural exchange · Read Takua Pa through the Baba cuisine, double-layered Tao Sor, and the Culture Str"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/food-people/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/food-people/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "หน้าแรก", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "อาหารบาบ๋าตะกั่วป่า รสชาติของชุมชนตลาดเก่า", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/food-people/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/food-people/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/food-people/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/food-people/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">เรื่องเล่า</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">แผ่นพับ</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/food-people/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>People and flavors</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · People and Flavors</span><h1>Flavours of the Baba Community and the Old Market</h1><p class="lead">Food serves as both household memories and evidence of cultural exchange · Read Takua Pa through the Baba cuisine, double-layered Tao Sor, and the Culture Street</p><p class="story-meta">Updated 2026-09-07 · 7 min read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2 is-illustration"><img src="/assets/food-center.webp" alt="ศูนย์อาหารลานโล่งตะกั่วป่า" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Open-Air Food Center</span><span class="credit">Illustration · No licensed photo available for this subject yet</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>A town that was once a tin mining hub and a port on the trans-peninsular route like Takua Pa has left behind more than just traces of Sino-Portuguese buildings or the operating hours of local markets. It also hides its own stories in the flavours and the way of life of its people.</p><p>In official documents, Takua Pa appears as a community that grew from integrating external cultures. Each dish acts as a reflection of settlement migration, cross-cultural marriages, and the changes in commercial districts through different eras. The flavours of today are edible memories.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">Takua Pa on the Baba Food Map</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/culture-street.webp" alt="ถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>When discussing the food culture on the Andaman coast, the Department of Cultural Promotion officially listed Takua Pa as part of the Baba-Peranakan food communities since its registration as a national cultural heritage in 2013 (2556 BE). This inclusion positions Takua Pa within the network of overseas Chinese descendants who arrived to mine tin and settled down with the locals.</p><p>This knowledge of flavours and cooking methods did not originate from any single isolated group. Instead, it emerged from passing down memories and culinary techniques within families. It is about preserving the identity and taste from generation to generation, whether it is a savoury dish in a regular meal or sweets for important family rituals.</p><p>However, placing Takua Pa on this food culture map serves to explain the overall cultural context. It does not mean that every household or shop in Takua Pa shares the exact same recipe for Moo Hong (braised pork belly), O-tao (fried oyster with taro), or Ang Ku (red tortoise cake). The diversity within each household remains the heart of the local food's vitality.</p></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Reading food menus or observing the local ingredients used in the market is a natural and gradual way of getting to know the way of life and flavours of Takua Pa.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">Double-layered Tao Sor and the Town's Transformation</h2><p>Among the frequently mentioned local foods, Tao Sor (flaky pastry) is a name that clearly appears in government records. The Phang Nga Provincial Tourism and Sports Office notes that Tao Sor is both a famous souvenir and an OTOP (One Tambon One Product) item of Takua Pa, describing it as similar to Chinese mooncakes but smaller, and often topped with sesame seeds.</p><p>The enduring presence of Tao Sor in Takua Pa was also recorded by the Food and Drug Administration during a field inspection in August 2025. It was documented that Tuang Rat shop continues the tradition of making Tao Sor using the traditional double-layered dough, an effort that earned them the FDA Quality Award for outstanding health products in the grassroots economy promotion category.</p><p>The story of Tao Sor helps us see how local sweets can develop and grow alongside the town's economy. At the same time, we cannot jump to the conclusion that Tao Sor originated in Takua Pa as the first in the world, or that there is only one monopolised original recipe here. This is because there is no historical evidence that clearly confirms its exact origin.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">Origin and original recipe are not settled</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">Culture Street and the Old Commercial District</h2><p>Stepping onto Si Takua Pa Road and Udom Thara Road, we are entering the former commercial centre of the tin mining era. Traces of this golden age are still visible through the architecture of shophouses with Straits colonial influences, featuring covered walkways at the front. Today, these streets have been repurposed into the Takua Pa Old Town Culture Street to draw people back to experience life in the old district.</p><p>The Department of Internal Trade's registry notes that this market is a gathering place for traditional sweets, local food, handicrafts, wickerwork, and cultural activities. The once-sleeping old streets are awakened every Sunday afternoon, creating a space where the younger generation and the original locals can meet and converse through shops and food.</p><p>However, the starting point of these activities still has information that is not entirely agreed upon. Some local news sources state that the retrospective event started in 2007, while official market registries list its founding and promotion in 2017. This discrepancy in information could be interpreted as referring to different milestones, between the community-initiated event and the official registration as a state-promoted market.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-disputed">Starting year of the event has conflicting sources</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">Lan Long Market, A Meeting Point of Diversity</h2><p>In addition to the walking street that opens once a week, Takua Pa also has a food hub ready to accommodate people every day, such as the Takua Pa Lan Long Food Centre. This place gathers various main dishes, ranging from rice and curry, made-to-order meals, Khanom Chin (fermented rice noodles), and noodles, to a complete selection of desserts and beverages.</p><p>These places are not just venues for buying and selling goods, but also serve as spaces for the townspeople to exchange conversations. Reading handwritten food menus or observing the local ingredients used in the market is a natural and gradual way of getting to know the way of life and flavours of Takua Pa.</p><p>As for information regarding the food centre's operating hours or the status of specific shops, data on the internet can be inaccurate and is not always updated in real-time. Asking locals on foot or checking directly with the vendors before your trip is a way to ensure you do not miss the chance to taste your favourite dishes.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">Operating hours and prices may change according to the season</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">Siwara Cafe in the Town's Storytelling System</h2><p>If you visit Takua Pa today, you might see many coffee shops and cafes attempting to present the town's story through their drinks and decor. Siwara Cafe is one of them, and is also the creator of this Takua Pa 101 information. The website has specifically categorised the cafe under the beverage shop section.</p><p>This categorisation and explanation are done so that readers can clearly, transparently, and without confusion separate the role of the information creators from historical facts. As for operating hours, new menus, or prices, readers can check the latest details directly through the shop's communication channels.</p><p>The growth of coffee shops and sitting areas today is a reflection of building upon the drinking and eating culture of the past. It blends with the lifestyles of the younger generation and locals who participate in revitalising the old district, bringing it back to life.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Takua Pa Open-Air Food Center</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="food-center">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="culture-street">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><aside class="story-outro"><p>บทความนี้เล่าที่มาของรสชาติ ส่วนรายชื่อร้านที่ยังเปิดอยู่จริงพร้อมเวลาเปิดและพิกัด อยู่ในคู่มือกินเที่ยวที่คนทำเว็บนี้เดินไปชิมมาเอง</p><a class="text-link" href="https://siwaracafe.com/guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เปิดคู่มือกินเที่ยวตะกั่วป่า ↗</a></aside><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The exact person who invented the first Tao Sor recipe in Takua Pa cannot be confirmed by printed documents.</li><li>The exact and most updated operating hours of markets and shops today.</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>National Intangible Cultural Heritage: Baba Food</cite> · กรมส่งเสริมวัฒนธรรม (Department of Cultural Promotion) · Page 124 (print) / PDF page 133 · <a href="https://book.culture.go.th/newbook/ich/ich2013.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Field news on Phang Nga Tao Sor pastry products</cite> · สำนักงานคณะกรรมการอาหารและยา (Food and Drug Administration) · 2025 (2568 BE) field news · <a href="https://dis.fda.moph.go.th/detail-newsUpdate?id=3271" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Phang Nga Local Products</cite> · สำนักงานการท่องเที่ยวและกีฬาจังหวัดพังงา (Phang Nga Provincial Tourism and Sports Office) · Takua Pa Tao Sor pastry · <a href="https://phangnga.mots.go.th/news/369" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Thanon Watthanatham Mueang Kao Takua Pa</cite> · กรมการค้าภายใน (Department of Internal Trade) · History · <a href="https://www.dit.go.th/th/market/2383/ภาคใต/พ-งงา/ถนนว-ฒนธรรมเม-องเก-าตะก-วป-า/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Experience the Cultural Street</cite> · ผู้จัดการออนไลน์ (Manager Online) · News content referencing 2007 (2550 BE) · <a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9650000044214" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Lan Long Market</cite> · Wongnai · General information · <a href="https://www.wongnai.com/attractions/399190Vq-ตลาดลานโล่ง" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Lan Long Food Court</cite> · เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality) · Video description Sep 1, 2024 (2567 BE) · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YBj0I_sMc-I" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">Tao Sor pastry originated in Takua Pa and has only one recipe</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">There is no historical evidence clearly stating the exact first origin or establishing a monopoly of a single recipe in Takua Pa. <a href="https://dis.fda.moph.go.th/detail-newsUpdate?id=3271" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The Culture Street event started in 2007 or 2017</div><div class="ev-status disputed">DISPUTED</div><div class="ev-explain">Some news sources cite 2007 for the beginning of the community's retrospective event, but the Department of Internal Trade documents state 2017 as a promoted market. <a href="https://www.dit.go.th/th/market/2383/ภาคใต/พ-งงา/ถนนว-ฒนธรรมเม-องเก-าตะก-วป-า/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">Takua Pa on the Baba Food Map</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">Double-layered Tao Sor and the Town's Transformation</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">Culture Street and the Old Commercial District</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">Lan Long Market, A Meeting Point of Diversity</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">Siwara Cafe in the Town's Storytelling System</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\stories\city\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>History of Takua Pa: The Town That Moved · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="From an ancient settlement on the trans-peninsular route to a market and mining town · Verified facts and questions waiting to be answered."><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="History of Takua Pa: The Town That Moved"><meta property="og:description" content="From an ancient settlement on the trans-peninsular route to a market and mining town · Verified facts and questions waiting to be answered."><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/city/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "หน้าแรก", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "ประวัติตะกั่วป่า เมืองที่ย้ายตามสายน้ำ", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/city/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">เรื่องเล่า</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">แผ่นพับ</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/city/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>Know the city</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · About the Town</span><h1>Takua Pa: The Town That Moved with the River</h1><p class="lead">From an ancient settlement on the trans-peninsular route to a market and mining town · Verified facts and questions waiting to be answered.</p><p class="story-meta">Updated on 2026-09-07 · 6-minute read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2 is-illustration"><img src="/assets/riverwalk.webp" alt="ตลาดริมน้ำตะกั่วป่า" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Takuapa Riverwalk</span><span class="credit">Illustration · No licensed photo available for this subject yet</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>If you have a chance to take a walk in Takua Pa old town on a holiday afternoon, you will see old houses, long streets, and a quiet, charming atmosphere. But deep beneath the cracks in the old walls and the slow-flowing river, this town hides a story of major transformation.</p><p>Takua Pa was not located here from the beginning, and it did not always look like this. The town is a living entity that grew, expanded, and moved in rhythm with its resources and the river. From a stopover point on the trans-peninsular route, it became a bustling major market town, and later a town that had to find a new home when the river was no longer favorable.</p><p>We will take you to read Takua Pa through what remains: the governor's residence wall that tells a story of wealth, the old temple by the canal, and the river that was once its main artery. This is a town with more than one version of history.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">The Ongoing Debate Over the Town's Name</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/wat-kongkha.webp" alt="วัดคงคาภิมุข (วัดคงคา)" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>When speaking of Takua Pa, the first question many people wonder is where the town's name came from. Official sources and historians still offer different explanations, and to date, there is no evidence that can bring this to a single, settled conclusion.</p><p>The first explanation links the town's name to the word "Takola", which appears in ancient records as a major port on the trans-peninsular route. This word is interpreted as a possible root that gradually changed in pronunciation to the current name over several centuries.</p><p>Another explanation is based on local geography and resources. It suggests that the name comes from combining the word "Takua" (which means tin, a mineral abundantly found in the area) and "Pa" (which means forest, reflecting the rich natural environment surrounding the town).</p><p>Although documents from the Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning (ONEP) list both explanations as basic information, they clearly emphasize that there is no definitive evidence to confirm either one. The town's name remains the first mystery greeting visitors, leaving us to imagine the past through different explanations.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The origin of the town's name is not settled</span></div></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Takua Pa was not located here from the beginning, and it did not always look like this. The town is a living entity that grew, expanded, and moved in rhythm with its resources and the river.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">The Governor's Residence Wall and Town Defense</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/governor-wall.webp" alt="กำแพงจวนเจ้าเมืองตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Governor’s Residence Wall</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The image of Takua Pa's past wealth is inextricably linked to the tin mining business. One of the structures that most clearly reflects the power and context of that era is the Takua Pa governor's residence wall, which stands as a historical witness to this day.</p><p>Scholars have suggested, not settled, that this wall was built during the time of Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon) while he was the governor between 1840 (2383 BE) and 1881. The wall surrounding the residence was designed as a defensive camp. During an era when the town faced conflicts among Chinese groups linked to the massive benefits from tin mining, the wall served as both a home for the ruler and a fortress to maintain peace.</p><p>The wall's structure is highly stable, constructed with layers of mortar mixed with gravel and sand. The base was designed to be wider than the wall itself. The parts that remain intact today reach a height of about 5 meters. Inside the wall, the area was divided into the front palace, the main hall, and the rear palace, with connecting doors well-proportioned throughout.</p><p>The significance of this governor's residence also appears in historical records when King Chulalongkorn (Rama V) came to inspect the residence on May 28, 1890 (109 RS or 2433 BE). During the excavation by the Fine Arts Department in 1999, Chinese ceramics from the Qing Dynasty and European ceramics were found, providing evidence of Takua Pa's international trade connections.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 ">The construction year is suggested, not settled, based on the governor's tenure</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">A Town That Grew with the River</h2><p>The Takua Pa River is not just a backdrop for the town, but the main artery that has nourished life and trade since the pioneering era. Official documents explain that Takua Pa old town is an ancient settlement located by the river, and it prospered as a trade center on the trans-peninsular route.</p><p>The bustling water trade and tin mining led to the expansion of an area called Talat Yai (the big market) during the reign of King Rama III, which became an important economic center. Near the hustle and bustle of trade, Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the town's temple) or Wat Kongkha, is situated on the west bank of the Takua Pa canal, serving as the spiritual center for the townspeople.</p><p>The history of Wat Kongkha reflects the complexity of the old town's information. The cultural environment data states that this temple was built in 1762, while the archaeological database uses 1936 as the construction year for the beautiful southern style ordination hall. This hall is decorated with stucco floral patterns and Buddha images, which are still preserved today.</p><p>The differences in these sets of numbers are something we often find in local history. Explaining the town's antiquity through official documents only provides an overview of the settlement in the entire area. It does not mean that every building, every bridge, or every location in the present town has the same age dating back to the town's founding era.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 ">The age of the location and the age of the building might not be the same year</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">The Day the River Grew Shallow and the Town Had to Move</h2><p>But nature never stays still, and the river is always ready to change. As time passed, the river that built the town began to change its role. ONEP reports note the end of the golden age of Talat Yai, which was primarily caused by geography and changes in resources.</p><p>Long and continuous tin mining caused a massive amount of sand and sediment to flow down and accumulate in the river. The Takua Pa River, which used to be deep enough for cargo ships and passenger boats to navigate easily, gradually became shallower until large-scale navigation declined and became difficult.</p><p>Eventually, when the river was no longer favorable as a center for trade and transportation, moving the town's administrative center was inevitable. Official reports state that Takua Pa had to move its town center to an area called Yan Yao in 1913.</p><p>That relocation marked the end of Talat Yai's prosperity as a center, turning this area into an old town stopped in time with the past. It left behind Sino-Portuguese architecture and traces of development for future generations to experience and study.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 ">Reasons for the town relocation are based on ONEP reports</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">Reading the Town Today</h2><p>Today, Takua Pa old town has come back to life in a different form from the past. The waterfront by the canal, which used to be a port for transporting tin, has now been revived into the area of the Takua Pa riverside market. It becomes lively again from Saturday afternoon to evening, filled with smiles, the aroma of local food, and the chatter of locals.</p><p>The governor's residence wall, once strong, is no longer used to defend against enemies. Instead, it has become a public space open to visitors and is often used as a venue for traditional festivals or major town events. Meanwhile, Wat Kongkha Phimuk continues to function as an active temple with residing monks. It is a peaceful place for meditation and a historical site that is still breathing.</p><p>If you have the chance to walk along the cultural street and the river, what surrounds you are not just stagnant ruins of the past, but evidence of adaptation for survival. The town that moved with the river back then continues to flow and change along with the people today.</p><p>Before fully believing any numbers or legends of the town, we would like to invite you to read and observe this town carefully. The history of Takua Pa is a charming large book. Some pages might be missing due to time, and some pages are still being written by all of us.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Takuapa Riverwalk</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="riverwalk">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="wat-kongkha">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h4>Governor’s Residence Wall</h4><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="governor-wall">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The exact construction year of the governor's residence wall</li><li>Definitive evidence confirming the origin of Takua Pa's name</li><li>The reasons and builders of Wat Kongkha in its early days</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Takua Pa Old Town</cite> · สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · Takua Pa Old Town webpage · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Takua Pa Old Town Documents</cite> · สผ. (ONEP) · PDF pages 11-13, 55-56, 58 · <a href="https://nced.onep.go.th/opendata/2568/oldtown36_announce/takuapa.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Archaeological Site Database: Takua Pa Town</cite> · ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) · List of archaeological sites and suggested city walls · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">The town's name originated from 'Takola' or 'Takua and Pa'</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">ONEP documents list both explanations, but emphasize that there is no definitive evidence to confirm either one. <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The governor's residence wall was built between 1840 and 1881</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">This is suggested, not settled, based on the tenure of Governor Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon), and is not an exact year of construction. <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">Wat Kongkha Phimuk was built in 1762</div><div class="ev-status disputed">DISPUTED</div><div class="ev-explain">The cultural environment data states 1762, while the archaeological database uses 1936 as the construction year for the ordination hall. The age of the location and the remaining building might not align. <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">The Ongoing Debate Over the Town's Name</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">The Governor's Residence Wall and Town Defense</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">A Town That Grew with the River</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">The Day the River Grew Shallow and the Town Had to Move</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">Reading the Town Today</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>

